Definition 4.3 (Sampling Distribution) The sampling distribution of an estimator \hat\theta=\hat{\theta}(X_1,\ldots,X_n) is the probability distribution of \hat\theta induced by the joint distribution of (X_1,\ldots,X_n).




The properties 4.2 of an estimator, such as bias and standard error, are defined via this distribution.
In general, however, deriving an exact sampling distribution is hard, because:

It depends on the chosen statistical model for the data (the distribution of X_i).

The estimator \hat\theta may be a complicated/nonlinear function of the sample.

In a few special cases, however, we can derive the full sampling distribution of the estimator.







Proposition 4.2 (Normal Random Sample: Sampling Distribution of \bar{X}) If 
X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^2),
 then 
\bar{X} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\!\left(\mu,\ \frac{\sigma^2}{n}\right).











NoteProof






Proof 4.2. Averages are linear combinations: \bar X=\tfrac1n\sum_{i=1}^n X_i. By the properties of the normal distribution 3.5.1, a linear combination of independent Normal variables is Normal. Moreover, 
\mathrm{E}[\bar X]=\tfrac1n\sum \mathrm{E}[X_i]=\mu,\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(\bar X)=\tfrac{1}{n^2}\sum \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}.
 So \bar X\sim \mathcal N\!\left(\mu,\sigma^2/n\right).











Proposition 4.3 (Normal Random Sample: Sampling Distribution of S^2) If

X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^2),

then the scaled sample variance follows a chi-squared distribution: 
\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2} \;\sim\; \chi^2_{\,n-1}.





Under Normality, \bar X and S^2 are also independent. We will use this later, but its proof is not needed here.






Note 4.3: Optional derivation of the chi-squared result (not examinable)





The proof relies on the following property of the \chi^2_p distribution: For p i.i.d. standard normal random variables 
Z_1,\ldots, Z_p \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, 1)
 their squared sum is \chi^2_p distributed: 
\sum_{i=1}^p Z_i^2 \sim \chi^2_p.

The key idea in the following proof to express \dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2} as a sum of n-1 i.i.d. standard normal random variables.
Step 1 (Standardise and express S^2 as a residual sum of squares). Define the standardised variables

Y_i \;=\; \frac{X_i-\mu}{\sigma}\quad (i=1,\dots,n), \qquad
\text{so } Y_1,\dots,Y_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, 1).

Let \bar Y = \tfrac1n\sum_{i=1}^n Y_i = \dfrac{\bar X-\mu}{\sigma}. Then

\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar X)^2
= \sigma^2 \sum_{i=1}^n \bigl( Y_i-\bar Y \bigr)^2,

so

\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2}
= \sum_{i=1}^n \bigl( Y_i-\bar Y \bigr)^2.

Thus \dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2} is the sum of squares of residuals after projecting Y onto its mean.
The remaining steps are to manipulate \sum_{i=1}^n \bigl( Y_i-\bar Y \bigr)^2 as a sum of n-1 i.i.d. squared standard Normal random variables.
Step 2 (Define n-1 “difference of mean vs next observation” variables). For k=1,\dots,n-1, let \bar Y_k=\tfrac1k\sum_{i=1}^k Y_i and define

Z_k \;=\; \sqrt{\frac{k}{k+1}}\;\bigl(\bar Y_k - Y_{k+1}\bigr).

Each Z_k is a linear combination of independent \mathcal N(0,1), hence Normal. Also,

\mathrm{E}[Z_k]=0,\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(Z_k)=\frac{k}{k+1}\Bigl(\mathrm{Var}(\bar Y_k)+\mathrm{Var}(Y_{k+1})\Bigr)
=\frac{k}{k+1}\Bigl(\frac{1}{k}+1\Bigr)=1,

so Z_k\sim \mathcal N(0,1).
Step 3 (The Z_k are independent).
Fix 1\le j&lt;k\le n-1. Write Z_j=a_j(\bar Y_j-Y_{j+1}) with a_j=\sqrt{j/(j+1)}, and

Z_k=a_k(\bar Y_k-Y_{k+1}),\qquad a_k=\sqrt{\frac{k}{k+1}},\qquad
\bar Y_k=\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^k Y_i.

View each Z as a linear combination of the Y_i. The coefficients are:

for Z_j: a_j\cdot \frac1j on Y_1,\dots,Y_j; -a_j on Y_{j+1}; 0 otherwise;
for Z_k: a_k\cdot \frac1k on Y_1,\dots,Y_k; -a_k on Y_{k+1}; 0 otherwise.

Using independence and \mathrm{Var}(Y_i)=1,

\mathrm{Cov}(Z_j,Z_k)=\sum_{i=1}^n \text{(coeff of }Y_i\text{ in }Z_j)\times \text{(coeff of }Y_i\text{ in }Z_k).

Only i=1,\dots,j and i=j+1 overlap. Hence

\mathrm{Cov}(Z_j,Z_k)
=\sum_{i=1}^j \Bigl(a_j\frac1j\Bigr)\Bigl(a_k\frac1k\Bigr)
\;+\; \Bigl(-a_j\Bigr)\Bigl(a_k\frac1k\Bigr)
= a_ja_k\Bigl(\frac{j}{jk}-\frac{1}{k}\Bigr)=0.

Thus the Z_k are pairwise uncorrelated. Moreover, the vector (Z_1,\ldots,Z_{n-1}) is jointly Normal, because it is a linear transformation of the jointly Normal vector (Y_1,\ldots,Y_n). For jointly Normal variables, uncorrelatedness implies independence. Hence Z_1,\dots,Z_{n-1} are independent \mathcal N(0,1).
Step 4 (\tfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2}= \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} Z_k^2 ).
Define S_k=\sum_{i=1}^k (Y_i-\bar Y_k)^2 (so S_n=\sum_{i=1}^n (Y_i-\bar Y)^2). Write Y_i-\bar Y_{k+1} = (Y_i-\bar Y_k) + (\bar Y_k-\bar Y_{k+1}), use \sum_{i=1}^k (Y_i-\bar Y_k)=0 to kill the cross-term, and note \bar Y_{k+1}-\bar Y_k=\tfrac{Y_{k+1}-\bar Y_k}{k+1}.
Therefore, we have the recursive formula for S_k: 
S_{k+1} \;=\; S_k \;+\; \frac{k}{k+1}\,\bigl(\bar Y_k - Y_{k+1}\bigr)^2
\qquad (k=1,\dots,n-1).

By induction, 
S_n
=\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac{k}{k+1}\,\bigl(\bar Y_k - Y_{k+1}\bigr)^2
=\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} Z_k^2.

Conclusion From the previous step, we have shown

\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2}
=\sum_{i=1}^n (Y_i-\bar Y)^2
=\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} Z_k^2,

a sum of n-1 independent standard Normal squares, hence

\boxed{\;\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2} \sim \chi^2_{\,n-1}\;}.

4.2 Properties of Estimators
When we use a statistic to estimate a parameter, we want to judge how good an estimator is.
Three fundamental properties guide this assessment:

Bias: Does the estimator hit the right target on average?
Variance/Standard Error: How much does it fluctuate from sample to sample?
Mean Squared Error (MSE): A combined measure that balances both bias and variance.

In this section we first look at bias 4.4, variance 4.5, and standard error 4.6, then bring them together through the MSE 4.8.






TipRemark (Properties via Moments)






Remark 4.2 (Properties via Moments). In the previous section we introduced the sampling distribution 4.3 of an estimator.
The key idea for what follows is that we don’t need the entire distribution to study how good an estimator is. The main properties — bias, variance, standard error, and MSE — can all be expressed in terms of expectations (i.e. moments 1.12).
So in practice, we will usually only need to work with the first and second moments of \hat\theta, rather than its full sampling distribution.





4.2.1 Bias, Variance, and Standard Error







Definition 4.4 (Bias of an Estimator) The bias of an estimator \hat{\theta} for a parameter \theta is the difference between its expected value and the true parameter value:

\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] - \theta.


If \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = 0, the estimator is called unbiased.
Otherwise, it is biased: it over- or under-estimates \theta on average. Bias does not mean that every realised estimate lies on the same side of \theta.












Example 4.1 (Bias of the Sample Mean) Suppose X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathrm{Distribution}(\mu,\sigma^2) with mean \mu.
Show that the sample mean

\bar{X} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i

is an unbiased estimator of \mu.

   Solution 
We compute the expectation of \bar{X}:

\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}]
= \mathrm{E}\!\left[\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\right]
= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[X_i].

Since each X_i has mean \mu,

\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] = \frac{1}{n}(n\mu) = \mu.

Therefore,

\mathrm{Bias}(\bar{X})
= \mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] - \mu
= \mu - \mu = 0.

So the sample mean \bar{X} is an unbiased estimator of \mu.












Definition 4.5 (Variance of an Estimator) The variance measures how much the estimator fluctuates around its expectation:

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}\!\big[(\hat{\theta}-\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}])^2\big].

A smaller variance means the estimator is more concentrated and less “noisy.”











Example 4.2 (Variance of the Sample Mean) Suppose X_1,\ldots,X_n is a random sample such that \mathrm{Var}(X_i) = \sigma^2.
Show that 
\mathrm{Var}(\bar{X}) = \frac{\sigma^2}{n}.


   Solution 
Since the data X_i are independent and have common variance \sigma^2, we have

\mathrm{Var}(\bar{X})
= \frac{1}{n^2} \mathrm{Var}\left[\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\right] = \frac{1}{n^2} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{Var}(X_i)
= \frac{\sigma^2}{n}.

So as n increases, \bar{X} becomes more tightly clustered around \mu.












Example 4.3 (Bias of the Sample Variance) Suppose X_1,\ldots,X_n is a random sample such that 
\mathrm{E}[X_i]=\mu \quad \text{and} \quad \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\sigma^2&lt;\infty.
 Consider the class of estimators 
S_c^{2} \;=\; \frac{1}{c} \sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar X)^2.
 Find c such that S_c^{2} is unbiased for \sigma^2.

   Solution 

Note: In the following, we use the fact that, for any random variable Y, 
\mathrm{Var}(Y) = \mathrm{E}[Y^2] - \mathrm{E}[Y]^2 \Rightarrow \mathrm{E}[Y^2] = \mathrm{Var}(Y) + \mathrm{E}[Y]^2.


We seek c such that \mathrm{E}[S_c^{2}] = \sigma^2.
Using the computational identity 4.1, 
\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar X)^2 \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 \;-\; n\,\bar X^{\,2}.

Hence, taking expectations termwise, 
\mathrm{E}[X_i^2]=\sigma^2+\mu^2,\qquad
\mathrm{E}[\bar X^{\,2}]=\mathrm{Var}(\bar X)+\{\mathrm{E}[\bar X]\}^2=\frac{\sigma^2}{n}+\mu^2.
 Therefore 
\mathrm{E}\!\left[\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar X)^2\right]
= n(\sigma^2+\mu^2) - n\!\left(\frac{\sigma^2}{n}+\mu^2\right)
= (n-1)\sigma^2.
 It follows that 
\mathrm{E}[S_c^{2}] = \frac{(n-1)\sigma^2}{c}
 Unbiasedness \mathrm{E}[S_c^{2}]=\sigma^2 gives c=n-1.
Conclusion. The unbiased estimator is 
S^{2} \;=\; \frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar X)^2.






In the next visualisation we look at the bias of the naive sample variance:

\widetilde S^2_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar{X})^2.

From Example 4.3, the bias is

\mathrm{Bias}(\widetilde S^2_n) = -\frac{\sigma^2}{n}.

This means \widetilde S^2_n underestimates on average the true population variance \sigma^2.


Show Visualisation
Before resampling, predict where the distribution of \widetilde S^2_n will be centred relative to \sigma^2. Then compare small and large n: observe what happens to both the bias and the spread, and explain why these are different properties.
The right-hand panel shows that the sampling distribution of \widetilde S^2_n is centred below \sigma^2. As n increases, the bias shrinks (since -\sigma^2/n \to 0), but for small n the underestimation is quite visible. This sentence is also the text-only takeaway from the simulation.

addLegendTopRight = (svg, items, {
  padding = 8,
  gap = 10,                // you can keep this small now
  swatch = 17,
  fontFamily = bodyFont,
  fontSize = 12,
  corner = 10,             // distance from top-right
  bg = "var(--plot-bg)",
  border = "var(--plot-grid)"
} = {}) =&gt; {
  const root = d3.select(svg);

  // Build legend; park it off-screen so it still participates in layout.
  const g = root.append("g")
    .attr("class", "plot-legend")
    .attr("font-family", fontFamily)
    .attr("font-size", fontSize)
    .attr("pointer-events", "none")
    .attr("transform", "translate(-1000000,-1000000)");

  const rowGap = 8;
  const rows = g.selectAll("g.row")
    .data(items)
    .join("g")
      .attr("class", "row")
      .attr("transform", (d, i) =&gt; `translate(0, ${i * (fontSize + rowGap) + fontSize / 2})`);

  // swatch
  rows.append("line")
    .attr("x1", 0).attr("x2", swatch)
    .attr("y1", 0).attr("y2", 0)
    .attr("stroke", d =&gt; d.stroke)
    .attr("stroke-width", d =&gt; d.strokeWidth ?? 2)
    .attr("stroke-dasharray", d =&gt; d.dasharray ?? null)
    .attr("stroke-linecap", "butt");

  // label
  rows.append("text")
    .attr("x", swatch + gap)
    .attr("y", 0)
    .attr("dominant-baseline", "middle")
    .attr("fill", d =&gt; d.stroke)
    .attr("font-weight", "600")
    .text(d =&gt; d.label);

  const labels = rows.select("text");

  // Background (inserted now; sized after measurement)
  const bgRect = g.insert("rect", ":first-child")
    .attr("class", "legend-bg")
    .attr("rx", 8).attr("ry", 8)
    .attr("fill", bg)
    .attr("fill-opacity", 0.9)
    .attr("stroke", border);

  function place() {
    // 1) Ensure labels' *painted* left edge starts at swatch+gap
    let adjusted = false;
    labels.each(function () {
      const t = this;
      const b = t.getBBox();
      const desiredLeft = swatch + gap;   // in the row's coord system
      const overshoot = desiredLeft - b.x; // &gt;0 means glyph extends left
      if (overshoot &gt; 0.1) {
        const x = parseFloat(t.getAttribute("x")) || 0;
        t.setAttribute("x", x + overshoot);
        adjusted = true;
      }
    });

    // If we adjusted any label, schedule another frame so BBoxes settle
    if (adjusted) {
      requestAnimationFrame(place);
      return;
    }

    // 2) Size background to content
    const content = g.node().getBBox();
    if (!content || content.width === 0 || content.height === 0) {
      requestAnimationFrame(place);
      return;
    }

    const boxW = content.width + 2 * padding;
    const boxH = content.height + 2 * padding;

    bgRect
      .attr("x", content.x - padding)
      .attr("y", content.y - padding)
      .attr("width", boxW)
      .attr("height", boxH);

    // 3) Anchor to SVG viewBox top-right
    const vb = svg.viewBox?.baseVal;
    const vbx = vb?.x ?? 0;
    const vby = vb?.y ?? 0;
    const vbw = vb?.width
      ?? (+svg.getAttribute("width") || svg.getBBox().width || svg.getBoundingClientRect().width);

    const tx = Math.round(vbx + vbw - boxW - corner);
    const ty = Math.round(vby + corner);

    g.attr("transform", `translate(${tx}, ${ty})`);
  }

  requestAnimationFrame(place);
};







