This provides a language of degrees of belief: probability distributions describe how plausible different values of \theta are, given what we know.


6.1.2 The Bayesian Inference Procedure
The Bayesian approach to inference has three ingredients:

Prior — a probability distribution that captures what we believe about \theta before seeing the data.
Likelihood — the sampling model evaluated at the observed data, viewed as a function of \theta.
Posterior — the updated distribution for \theta that combines prior information with the evidence from the data.

The update is performed using Bayes’ theorem (introduced in the next section). Intuitively:

\text{Posterior} \;\; \propto \;\; \text{Likelihood} \times \text{Prior}.

This equation expresses the central idea: our beliefs about \theta are revised in light of the observed data.

Point Estimate: a single statistic \hat{\theta}(\underline{X}).
Interval estimate: two statistics L(\underline{X}) and U(\underline{X}) that form a random interval.








Definition 7.1 (Confidence Interval) A 100(1-\alpha)\% confidence interval for \theta is a random interval 
\big[L(\underline{X}),\; U(\underline{X})\big]
 constructed so that, for every parameter value \theta under consideration, 
\Pr_{\theta}\!\big(\theta\in[L(\underline{X}),U(\underline{X})]\big) \geq 1-\alpha.

The probability is over repeated samples generated at the fixed value \theta. This property is called coverage: in the long run, at least a proportion 1-\alpha of intervals constructed in this way will contain the true \theta. When equality holds, the interval has exact coverage 1-\alpha.






