Observe and explain. The region retains parameter values whose posterior density exceeds the threshold. For a requested credibility, \gamma is chosen so that the included posterior mass is 1-\alpha.






If the posterior distribution has several separated modes, then an HDR may be the union of several disjoint intervals. For example,

C_\alpha = (a,b)\cup(c,d)\cup(e,f), \quad a&lt;b&lt;c&lt;d&lt;e&lt;f.








Example 8.1 (Beta HDI) Suppose that the posterior distribution for \theta is a \mathrm{Beta}(1,24) distribution, with probability density function

\pi(\theta \mid \underline{x}) = 24(1-\theta)^{23}, \quad 0&lt;\theta&lt;1.

Determine the 100(1-\alpha)\% HDI for \theta.

   Solution 
The HDR must include those values of \theta with highest posterior density and so, up to endpoints of posterior probability zero, takes the form C_\alpha=[0,b]. The endpoint b must satisfy

\int_0^b 24(1-\theta)^{23}\,d\theta = 1-\alpha.

Now,

\int_0^b 24(1-\theta)^{23}\,d\theta
= \left[-(1-\theta)^{24}\right]^b_0 = 1-(1-b)^{24}.

Hence,

1-(1-b)^{24} = 1-\alpha
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
1-b=\alpha^{1/24}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
b=1-\alpha^{1/24}.

Therefore, a 100(1-\alpha)\% HDI for \theta is

[0,\, 1-\alpha^{1/24}].

For example, the 95\% HDI is approximately [0,0.117]. Conditional on the model, prior and data that produced this posterior, there is 0.95 posterior probability that \theta lies in this interval.












Example 8.2 (Normal HDI) Suppose we have a random sample \underline{x} from a \mathcal{N}(\mu,1/\tau) distribution (where \tau is known).
Suppose the posterior distribution is

\mu \mid \underline{x} \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\bar x,\; \tfrac{1}{n\tau}\right).

Determine the 100(1-\alpha)\% HDI for \mu.

   Solution 
This distribution has a symmetric bell shape and so the HDI takes the form C_\alpha=(a,b) with end-points

a=\bar x - \frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}}{\sqrt{n\tau}},
\qquad
b=\bar x + \frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}}{\sqrt{n\tau}},

where z_p=\Phi^{-1}(p) is the lower-tail p-quantile of the \mathcal{N}(0,1) distribution.
Therefore, the 95\% HDI for \mu is

\left(\bar x - \frac{1.96}{\sqrt{n\tau}},\;
\bar x + \frac{1.96}{\sqrt{n\tau}}\right).

Note that this interval is numerically identical to the 95% frequentist confidence interval for the (population) mean of a normal random sample with known variance.
However, the interpretation is very different.












Example 8.3 (Revisiting the Skeleton-Length Example) In Example 7.1, n=30, \bar{x}=161.72 cm and the sampling variance was assumed known to be \sigma^2=100 cm^2. Suppose a Bayesian analysis instead uses the prior 
\mu\sim\mathcal N(160,25).
 Find the posterior distribution and a 95\% credible interval.

Solution
Because \bar X\mid\mu\sim\mathcal N(\mu,100/30), Normal–Normal conjugacy gives 
V_{\mathrm{post}}
=\left(\frac{1}{25}+\frac{30}{100}\right)^{-1}
=2.941,
 and 
m_{\mathrm{post}}
=V_{\mathrm{post}}
\left(\frac{160}{25}+\frac{30(161.72)}{100}\right)
=161.518.
 Hence 
\mu\mid\underline{x}\sim\mathcal N(161.518,2.941),
 and the 95\% equal-tailed interval (also the HDI here) is 
161.518\pm1.96\sqrt{2.941}
=\boxed{[158.16,164.88]}\ \text{cm}.

Given the Normal model, this prior and the observed data, the posterior probability that \mu lies in this interval is 0.95. The frequentist interval in Example 7.1 is numerically similar, but it describes the repeated-sampling coverage of a procedure. The Bayesian interval is slightly pulled towards the prior mean 160 cm.











Chapter summary. Credible intervals make posterior probability statements conditional on a model, prior and observed data. A credibility level does not uniquely determine a set, so conventions such as equal-tailed intervals and highest-density regions are used. Confidence and credible intervals can occasionally share endpoints, but they answer different probability questions.

Important 9.1: Outcomes of a Hypothesis Test



Because hypothesis tests use random sample data, there is always a chance of reaching the wrong conclusion. A helpful way to understand this is to view the test as a diagnostic system — much like a fire alarm.

Starting Assumption:
A hypothesis test always begins by assuming the null hypothesis H_0 is true.
Triggering the Alarm:
We reject H_0 only when the data provide strong evidence against this assumption.

Analogy: Rejecting H_0 corresponds to “sounding the alarm”.


Given this interpretation, the interaction between the true state of nature and our decision leads to two kinds of errors.

The Two Errors


Type I Error: The False Alarm

Scenario: H_0 is actually true, but we decide to reject it.

Analogy: The fire alarm goes off, but there is no fire.

Consequence: We incorrectly claim evidence of an effect when none exists.

This error is controlled by choosing a significance level \alpha:

\Pr(\text{reject } H_0 \mid H_0 \text{ true})\leq\alpha.




Type II Error: The Missed Detection

Scenario: H_0 is actually false, but we fail to reject it.

Analogy: A fire is happening, but the alarm stays silent.

Consequence: We fail to detect a real effect.




Summary of Outcomes
The relationship between reality (the truth) and our decision is summarised below:












Truth: H_0 is True (Nothing is happening)
Truth: H_0 is False (Something is happening)




Decision: Reject H_0 (Sound Alarm)
Type I Error (False Alarm)
Correct Decision (Valid Detection)


Decision: Fail to Reject H_0 (Stay Quiet)
Correct Decision (Correctly ignored)
Type II Error (Missed Detection)





Table 9.1: Outcomes of a hypothesis test. The columns represent reality; the rows represent the statistical decision.












9.1.1 General Recipe
To construct a test of level \alpha for H_0 : \theta = \theta_0, we proceed as follows.

Specify the hypotheses.
Write down the simple null hypothesis 
H_0 : \theta = \theta_0,
 and choose an appropriate one-sided or two-sided alternative H_1.
Choose a test statistic.
Select a statistic T(\underline{X}) whose values tend to move in a predictable way when H_0 is false (for example, becoming large or small under H_1).
Find its distribution under H_0.
Determine (exactly or approximately) the sampling distribution of T(\underline{X}) assuming H_0 is true.
Define a rejection region R_\alpha.
For the chosen level \alpha, choose a set R_\alpha of “extreme” values of T such that 
\Pr\big(T \in R_\alpha \mid H_0\big) \leq \alpha,
 with equality when possible.
With a continuous test statistic and a simple null, this is usually straightforward:

a one-sided test uses one tail of the distribution of T under H_0,

a two-sided test uses both tails.

If T is discrete, the probability may be strictly less than \alpha, making the test conservative — although discrete test statistics are not examinable.
Compute the observed statistic.
From the data, calculate

T_{\text{obs}} = T(\underline{x}).

Decision rule.

If T_{\text{obs}} \in R_\alpha, reject H_0.

If T_{\text{obs}} \notin R_\alpha, fail to reject2 H_0.








Important 9.2: Ingredients of a Hypothesis Test





A hypothesis test is fully determined by two choices:

A test statistic T(\underline{X}) that captures evidence against H_0.

A rejection region R_\alpha describing which values of T count as “too inconsistent” with H_0.

There are usually many possible test statistics.
We prefer ones that are sensitive to departures from H_0 — that is, statistics that give high power.
Even once T is chosen, different rejection regions are possible.
By convention we choose regions containing the most extreme values of T in the direction specified by the alternative H_1, since this usually maximises power.
To construct R_\alpha, we need the sampling distribution 4.3 of T(\underline{X}) under H_0.
This distribution may be obtained exactly or approximated using asymptotic distributions 4.2.










Definition 9.2 (Rejection Region) Given a test statistic T(\underline{X}) and a chosen significance level \alpha, a rejection region is a subset R_\alpha of the possible values of T such that 
\Pr\big(T \in R_\alpha \mid H_0\big) \leq \alpha,
 with equality whenever this is possible.
This ensures that the probability of a Type I error (rejecting a true H_0) is at most \alpha.

With a simple null and a continuous test statistic, the equality above is usually achievable.

With a discrete statistic, the probability may be strictly less than \alpha, making the test conservative — although discrete test statistics are not examinable in this module.

In practice:

For a two-sided test, R_\alpha consists of both tails of the distribution of T under H_0.

For a one-sided test, R_\alpha is a single tail (upper or lower), depending on the direction of H_1.

Other choices of R_\alpha are mathematically possible, but by convention we use the most extreme values of T, because this typically maximises power.




As our first example of a hypothesis test, we will deliberately pick a bad choice of test statistic T and a couple of different rejection regions R_\alpha.







Example 9.1 (A Terrible Hypothesis Test) Suppose X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu, 1) and we wish to test

H_0: \mu = 0.
H_1: \mu \neq 0.

We choose T = X_1 as our test statistic.

For a chosen \alpha, determine two different rejection regions.

With the observed sample 
x_1=0.4,\; x_2=3.2,\; x_3=2.1,\; x_4=2.3,\; x_5=1.8,
 for which \alpha would you reject H_0?


Solution
a. Two possible rejection regions
Under H_0 note that T \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1).

Conventional two-sided test: Reject if T is very large in magnitude: 
R_\alpha^{(1)} = \{\, |T| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2} \,\}.

One-tail rule poorly matched to the stated two-sided alternative: Reject if T is very large and positive only: 
R_\alpha^{(2)} = \{\,T &gt; z_{1-\alpha}\,\}.
 This region also has probability \alpha under H_0, but it only detects positive departures from 0. It is therefore a valid level-\alpha rule but a poor test of the stated two-sided alternative.

There are infinitely many choices that are mathematically possible (e.g. a left-tail test, or even a symmetric band around 0 with total mass \alpha), but these would be poor at detecting the alternatives we care about.
b. Observed value
Here T_{\text{obs}} = x_1 = 0.4.

For the two-sided region R_\alpha^{(1)}: Reject if |0.4| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2}. This happens only if \alpha \geq 0.689.
For the one-sided region R_\alpha^{(2)}: Reject if 0.4 &gt; z_{1-\alpha}. This requires \alpha \geq 0.345.

So in both cases you would only reject H_0 at extremely large (non-standard) significance levels. At any usual choice like \alpha=0.05 or 0.01, you fail to reject.

Why this test is terrible (and instructive):

It ignores n-1 observations, so the power is extremely poor (see Example 9.2).
Different R_\alpha give different answers, showing that both T and R_\alpha matter.
The statistic X_1 is a high variance indicator of \mu it does not use the information in the full sample.







In Example 9.1 we were essentially asking:

“What is the smallest significance level \alpha at which the observed test statistic would fall inside the rejection region?”

This leads naturally to the definition of the p-value.







Definition 9.3 (p-value) Given an observed test statistic T_{\text{obs}}, the p-value is the smallest significance level \alpha for which T_{\text{obs}} would lie in the rejection region R_\alpha.
Equivalently, it is the probability, under H_0, of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as T_{\text{obs}}: 
p = \Pr \big( T \text{ is at least as extreme as } T_{\text{obs}} \mid H_0 \big).


For a two-sided test, “extreme” means very large or very small values of T.

For a one-sided test, “extreme” refers only to the tail specified by H_1.











What a p-value is not. It is not \Pr(H_0\mid\underline{x}), the probability that the result occurred “by chance,” or a measure of effect size. It measures how extreme the observed statistic is under the null model. Choose \alpha and the direction of H_1 before inspecting the result; changing them afterwards invalidates the advertised Type I error control.



Almost all tests in practice are either one-sided or two-sided.
Restricting to these two cases fixes the shape of the rejection region.







Definition 9.4 (One-Sided and Two-Sided Tests)  

In a one-sided test, the alternative hypothesis H_1 specifies a direction.

Example: H_0 : \mu = \mu_0 vs. H_1 : \mu &gt; \mu_0. The rejection region then takes the form 
R_\alpha = \{\, T \mid T \ge c_\alpha \,\},
 so we only reject for large values of T.
If the alternative is H_1 : \mu &lt; \mu_0, the rejection region is 
R_\alpha = \{\, T \mid T \le c_\alpha \,\},
 so we only reject for small values of T.

In a two-sided test, the alternative hypothesis does not specify a direction
(e.g. H_1 : \mu \neq \mu_0).

The rejection region lies in both tails: 
R_\alpha = \{\, T \mid |T| \ge c_\alpha \,\}.

The total significance level \alpha is usually split between the two tails, giving probability \alpha/2 to each under H_0.












TipRemark (Why these regions?)






Remark 9.2 (Why these regions?). For a given test statistic, there are infinitely many possible rejection regions of level \alpha. In practice we choose the regions above because:

they contain the most extreme values of T in the direction(s) where H_1 differs from H_0;

such regions typically give the test the highest power against the alternatives we care about.

So these rejection regions arise not from necessity, but because they are the most effective.






9.1.2 Power of a Test
Recall from Table 9.1 that a hypothesis test may commit two types of error:

a Type I error (rejecting a true H_0),

a Type II error (failing to reject a false H_0).

To study both errors within a single framework, we introduce the power function, which records the probability that the test rejects H_0 at each possible parameter value.







Definition 9.5 (Power Function) At each parameter value \theta, define the non-rejection probability and power by 
\beta(\theta)=\Pr_\theta(T\notin R_\alpha),
\qquad
\pi(\theta)=1-\beta(\theta)=\Pr_\theta(T\in R_\alpha).


If \theta \in H_0, \pi(\theta) is the Type I error probability, which by construction is at most \alpha.
If \theta \in H_1, \beta(\theta) is the Type II error probability and \pi(\theta) is the probability of correctly rejecting H_0 at that value of \theta.

Thus the power function \pi(\theta) links both types of error in a single quantity: it should be small on H_0 and ideally large on H_1.





High power means the test is good at detecting departures from H_0.

High specificity means the test avoids false alarms (low Type I error).

In practice there is a trade-off: reducing \alpha lowers the chance of a Type I error but usually decreases power.







Example 9.2 (The Power of a Terrible Hypothesis Test) Continuing from Example 9.1, for each of the rejection regions

R_\alpha^{(1)} = \{\, |T| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2} \,\},

R_\alpha^{(2)} = \{\, T &gt; z_{1-\alpha} \,\},

derive the power functions of the test as a function of \mu.

Solution
The alternative hypothesis is H_1 : \mu \neq 0.
Under this alternative, the test statistic has distribution 
T = X_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, 1).


1. Two-sided region

R_\alpha^{(1)} = \{\, |T| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2} \,\}.

We reject when the absolute value of the statistic is large. The power is

\Pr(|T| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2})
= \Pr(T &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2}) + \Pr(T &lt; -z_{1-\alpha/2}).

Standardise using Z = T - \mu, where Z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1): 
\Pr(T &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2}) = \Pr(Z &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2} - \mu),

\Pr(T &lt; -z_{1-\alpha/2}) = \Pr(Z &lt; -z_{1-\alpha/2} - \mu).

Hence the power function is 
\Pr(|T| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2})
= 1 - \Phi(z_{1-\alpha/2} - \mu)
  + \Phi(-z_{1-\alpha/2} - \mu).


2. One-sided region

R_\alpha^{(2)} = \{\, T &gt; z_{1-\alpha} \,\}.

Here we reject only for large positive values of the statistic. The power is

\Pr(T &gt; z_{1-\alpha})
  = \Pr(Z &gt; z_{1-\alpha} - \mu)
  = 1 - \Phi(z_{1-\alpha} - \mu).


Summary
For the two-sided test, 
\pi(\mu)
= 1 - \big[ \Phi(z_{1-\alpha/2} - \mu)
            - \Phi(-z_{1-\alpha/2} - \mu) \big].

For the one-sided test, 
\pi(\mu)
= 1 - \Phi(z_{1-\alpha} - \mu).

Both satisfy \pi(0) = \alpha when \mu = 0, as required for an exact level-\alpha test.







