c. Maximum likelihood estimate.
Differentiate the log-likelihood:

\frac{\partial}{\partial b}\ell(b;\underline{x}) = \frac{n}{b} - \sum_{i=1}^n x_i.

Set equal to 0:

\frac{n}{b} = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i \quad \Rightarrow \quad \hat{b} = \frac{1}{\bar{x}}.

So the MLE is \hat{b} = 1/\bar{x}.









ImportantDeriving the Student-t distribution



Let X_1,\ldots, X_n \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^2). From Proposition 4.2 and Proposition 4.3, we saw that

\bar{X} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2/n),
\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2} \;\sim\; \chi^2_{\,n-1}, and
The sample variance S^2 was independent of the sample mean \bar{X}.

Derive the PDF of the random variable 
T = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu}{S / \sqrt{n}}.



Solution

Set 
Z \;=\; \frac{\bar{X}-\mu}{\sigma/\sqrt{n}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1),
\qquad
U \;=\; \frac{(n-1)S^2}{\sigma^2} \sim \chi^2_{\,n-1},
 with Z independent of U. Then 
T \;=\; \frac{\sigma Z}{\sqrt{(\sigma^2 U)/(n-1)}} \;=\; \frac{Z}{\sqrt{U/\nu}},
\qquad \nu := n-1.

Conditional distribution. Conditioning on U=u, 
T \mid U=u \;=\; \frac{Z}{\sqrt{u/\nu}}
\;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\!\left(0,\; \frac{\nu}{u}\right),
 so its conditional density is 
f_{T\mid U}(t\mid u)
= \sqrt{\frac{u}{2\pi\nu}}\;\exp\!\left(-\frac{u}{2\nu}t^2\right), \qquad t\in\mathbb{R}.

Mixture over U. The marginal PDF of T is 
f_T(t)
= \int_0^\infty f_{T\mid U}(t\mid u)\, f_U(u)\, \mathrm{d}u,

where f_U(u) = \dfrac{1}{2^{\nu/2}\Gamma(\nu/2)}\,u^{\nu/2-1} e^{-u/2} for u&gt;0. Thus
\begin{align*}
f_T(t)
&= \int_0^\infty
\left[\sqrt{\frac{u}{2\pi\nu}}\;e^{-\frac{u}{2\nu}t^2}\right]
\left[\frac{1}{2^{\nu/2}\Gamma(\nu/2)}\,u^{\nu/2-1} e^{-u/2}\right] \mathrm{d}u \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\nu}\,2^{\nu/2}\Gamma(\nu/2)}
\int_0^\infty
u^{\frac{\nu+1}{2}-1}
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{1}{2}\!\left(1+\frac{t^2}{\nu}\right)u\right\}
\,\mathrm{d}u.
\end{align*}
Use the Gamma integral \displaystyle \int_0^\infty u^{a-1} e^{-b u}\,\mathrm{d}u = \Gamma(a)\,b^{-a} with a=\frac{\nu+1}{2} and b=\frac{1}{2}\!\left(1+\frac{t^2}{\nu}\right):
\begin{align*}
f_T(t)
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\nu}\,2^{\nu/2}\Gamma(\nu/2)}
\cdot \Gamma\!\left(\frac{\nu+1}{2}\right)
\left[\frac{1}{2}\!\left(1+\frac{t^2}{\nu}\right)\right]^{-\frac{\nu+1}{2}} \\
&= \frac{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\nu+1}{2}\right)}{\sqrt{\nu\pi}\,\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\nu}{2}\right)}
\left(1+\frac{t^2}{\nu}\right)^{-\frac{\nu+1}{2}}, \qquad t\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{align*}
This is the Student-t density with \nu=n-1 degrees of freedom: 
T \sim t_\nu \quad \text{with} \quad
f_T(t) = \frac{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\nu+1}{2}\right)}{\sqrt{\nu\pi}\,\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\nu}{2}\right)}
\left(1+\frac{t^2}{\nu}\right)^{-\frac{\nu+1}{2}}.










ImportantSample Mean and Standard Deviation



Emergency admissions to 10 hospitals were recorded on a particular day.
The summary statistics were:

n = 10

\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = 207

\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 = 4501

Find the sample mean \bar{x} and the sample standard deviation s.


Solution

The sample mean is 
\bar{x} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = \frac{207}{10} = 20.7.

To find the sample variance, 
s^2 = \frac{1}{n-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 - n \bar{x}^2\right).

Substituting the values: 
s^2 = \frac{1}{9}\left(4501 - 10 \times (20.7)^2\right)
= \frac{1}{9}(4501 - 4284.9)
= 24.011.

Taking the square root gives the sample standard deviation: 
s = \sqrt{24.011} \approx 4.900.










Important 5: Central Limit Theorem Approximation



The scores that responders give on a questionnaire can be assumed to be independent random variables, each with the following probability distribution:



Score
1
2
3
4
5




Probability
0.3
0.1
0.2
0.1
0.3



Use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to approximate the probability that the average score from n = 100 responders lies in the range (2.8,\,3.2).


Solution

Let X be the score for a randomly selected responder.
We first compute the moments.
Expected value: 
\mathrm{E}[X] = \sum_{i=1}^5 i \Pr(X=i)
= 1(0.3) + 2(0.1) + 3(0.2) + 4(0.1) + 5(0.3) = 3.

Second moment: 
\mathrm{E}[X^2] = \sum_{i=1}^5 i^2 \Pr(X=i)
= 1^2(0.3) + 2^2(0.1) + 3^2(0.2) + 4^2(0.1) + 5^2(0.3) = 11.6.

Variance: 
\operatorname{Var}(X) = \mathrm{E}[X^2] - (\mathrm{E}[X])^2 = 11.6 - 9 = 2.6.


Let \bar{X} be the sample mean. By the Central Limit Theorem, 
\bar{X} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\!\left(3,\; \frac{2.6}{100}\right).

We want: 
\Pr(2.8 &lt; \bar{X} &lt; 3.2)
= \Pr\!\left(\frac{2.8-3}{\sqrt{2.6/100}} &lt; Z &lt; \frac{3.2-3}{\sqrt{2.6/100}}\right),
 where Z \sim N(0,1).
Compute the bounds: 
\frac{2.8-3}{\sqrt{0.026}} = -1.24,
\qquad
\frac{3.2-3}{\sqrt{0.026}} = 1.24.

Thus, 
\Pr(-1.24 &lt; Z &lt; 1.24)
= \Pr(Z &lt; 1.24) - \Pr(Z &lt; -1.24).

Using R, Python or referring to the standard normal tables: 
\Pr(Z &lt; 1.24) = 0.8925, \qquad \Pr(Z &lt; -1.24) = 0.1075.

So, 
\Pr(2.8 &lt; \bar{X} &lt; 3.2) \approx 0.8925 - 0.1075 = 0.785.










Important 6: Normal Approximation to the Binomial



Use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to justify the Normal approximation to the Binomial Binomial.
That is, if X \sim \mathrm{Bin}(n,p), show that for large n (with p \neq 0,1), 
X \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N} \big(np,\; np(1-p)\big).



Solution

The Binomial distribution counts the number of successes in n independent Bernoulli trials.
For trial i, define the indicator 
I_i =
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if success}, \\
0, & \text{if failure}.
\end{cases}

Each I_i has 
\mathrm{E}[I_i] = p, \qquad \operatorname{Var}(I_i) = p(1-p).

The total number of successes is 
X = \sum_{i=1}^n I_i.

By the Central Limit Theorem, the sum of independent, identically distributed random variables is approximately Normal when n is large. Therefore, 
X \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N} \big(np,\; np(1-p)\big).










Important 7: Properties of an Estimator I



Suppose X_1, X_2, X_3 are independent random variables, each with expectation
\mathrm{E}[X] = 2\theta + 1 and variance \operatorname{Var}(X) = \theta^2/12, where \theta is an unknown parameter.
Consider the estimator \hat{\theta} of \theta, where 
\hat{\theta} = 2X_1 - X_2 + X_3.

Answer the following:

Find the expectation of the estimator \hat{\theta}.

Find the variance of the estimator \hat{\theta}.

Determine the bias of the estimator \hat{\theta}.



Solution

(a) Expectation 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] = \mathrm{E}[2X_1 - X_2 + X_3]
= 2\mathrm{E}[X_1] - \mathrm{E}[X_2] + \mathrm{E}[X_3].
 Since each X_i has the same mean \mathrm{E}[X] = 2\theta + 1, 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] = 2(2\theta+1) - (2\theta+1) + (2\theta+1) = 4\theta + 2.


(b) Variance 
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) = \operatorname{Var}(2X_1 - X_2 + X_3).
 Since the X_i are independent, 
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) = 4\operatorname{Var}(X_1) + \operatorname{Var}(X_2) + \operatorname{Var}(X_3).
 Each has variance \theta^2/12, so 
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) = (4+1+1)\frac{\theta^2}{12} = \frac{\theta^2}{2}.


(c) Bias 
\operatorname{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] - \theta = (4\theta + 2) - \theta = 3\theta + 2.










Important 8: Properties of an Estimator II



Suppose X_1, X_2, X_3, X_4 are independent random variables, each with
\mathrm{E}[X] = 3\theta and \operatorname{Var}(X) = \theta^2/9, where \theta is an unknown parameter.
Consider the estimator 
\hat{\theta} = X_1 + 2X_2 - X_3 + X_4.

Answer the following:

Find the expectation of the estimator \hat{\theta}.

Find the variance of the estimator \hat{\theta}.

Determine the bias of the estimator \hat{\theta}.



Solution

(a) Expectation 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] = \mathrm{E}[X_1] + 2\mathrm{E}[X_2] - \mathrm{E}[X_3] + \mathrm{E}[X_4].
 Each has mean 3\theta, so 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] = 3\theta + 2(3\theta) - 3\theta + 3\theta = 9\theta.


(b) Variance 
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) = \operatorname{Var}(X_1) + 4\operatorname{Var}(X_2) + \operatorname{Var}(X_3) + \operatorname{Var}(X_4).
 Each has variance \theta^2/9, so 
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) = (1+4+1+1)\frac{\theta^2}{9} = \frac{7\theta^2}{9}.


(c) Bias 
\operatorname{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] - \theta = 9\theta - \theta = 8\theta.










Important 9: Properties of an Estimator III



Suppose X_1, X_2, X_3 are independent random variables, each with

\mathrm{E}[X_i] = \theta - 5,
\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(X_i) = 2\theta^2,
 where \theta is an unknown parameter.
Consider the estimator 
\hat{\theta} = X_1 + 2X_2 - 4X_3.

Answer the following:

Find the expectation of the estimator \hat{\theta}.

Find the variance of the estimator \hat{\theta}.

Determine the bias of the estimator \hat{\theta}.



Solution

(a) Expectation
Using linearity of expectation: 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]
= \mathrm{E}[X_1] + 2\,\mathrm{E}[X_2] - 4\,\mathrm{E}[X_3]
= (\theta - 5) + 2(\theta - 5) - 4(\theta - 5)
= -\,(\theta - 5)
= 5 - \theta.


(b) Variance
Because X_1, X_2, X_3 are independent, 
\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta})
= \mathrm{Var}(X_1) + 4\,\mathrm{Var}(X_2) + 16\,\mathrm{Var}(X_3)
= (1 + 4 + 16)\,2\theta^2
= 42\theta^2.


(c) Bias
The bias is 
\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})
= \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] - \theta
= (5 - \theta) - \theta
= 5 - 2\theta.

Hence the estimator is biased, and the bias depends linearly on \theta.









Important 10: Properties of the Poisson distribution



A hospital records the number of patients arriving at the emergency department per hour.
Suppose X \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda), where \lambda is the (unknown) mean arrival rate.

Compute \mathrm{E}[X] and \mathrm{Var}(X).
We estimate \lambda by the sample mean \hat{\lambda} = \tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i from n independent hours of observation.
Show that \mathrm{E}[\hat{\lambda}] = \lambda and compute \mathrm{Var}(\hat{\lambda}).
Suppose in one hour you observe x=7 arrivals.

Compute the estimate \hat{\lambda}_{\text{obs}}.

Using \hat{\lambda}_{\text{obs}} as \lambda, compute the pmf value p(X=7 \mid \hat{\lambda}_{\text{obs}}).

Compute F(7 \mid \lambda = \hat{\lambda}_\text{obs} = \mathrm{Pr}(X \leq 7 \mid \hat{\lambda}_\text{\text{obs}}) using \hat{\lambda}_{\text{obs}}.




Solution


For X \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda), 
\mathrm{E}[X] = \lambda, \quad \mathrm{Var}(X) = \lambda.

If \hat{\lambda} = \tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i, then 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\lambda}] = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[X_i] = \lambda,
 so \hat{\lambda} is unbiased.
Since \mathrm{Var}(X_i) = \lambda and the X_i are independent, 
\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\lambda}) = \frac{\lambda}{n}.

With x=7,

\hat{\lambda}_{\text{obs}} = 7.

The PMF value is 
\mathrm{Pr}(X=7 \mid \lambda=7) = \frac{7^7 e^{-7}}{7!}.

The CDF value is 
\mathrm{Pr}(X \leq 7 \mid \lambda=7) = \sum_{k=0}^7 \frac{7^k e^{-7}}{k!}.



In Python:


Show code
from scipy.stats import poisson

lambd_hat = 7
part_ii = poisson.pmf(k = 7, mu=lambd_hat) # pmf value at x = 7
part_iii = poisson.cdf(k = 7, mu=lambd_hat) # cdf value at x = 7

print(f"Part (ii) answer: {part_ii} \nPart (iii) answer: {part_iii}")


Part (ii) answer: 0.14900277967433773 
Part (iii) answer: 0.5987138355230368











ImportantVariance of an Estimator (with dependence)



Let X_1,\dots,X_n be random variables with the same variance and same pairwise covariance: 
\operatorname{Var}(X_i)=\sigma^2,\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)=\tau\quad (i\neq j),
 where \sigma^2&gt;0 and \tau are known constants (no independence is assumed).
For fixed weights a_1,\dots,a_n\in\mathbb{R}, consider the linear estimator 
\hat{\theta}=\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i.

Compute \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta}) in terms of {a_i}, \sigma^2, and \tau.


Solution

Using \operatorname{Var}\left(\sum_i a_i X_i\right)=\sum_i\sum_j a_i a_j\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j) and the structure of the covariances: 
\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta})
= \sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 \sigma^2 + \sum_{i\neq j} a_i a_j \tau
= \sigma^2\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 + \tau \left[\Big(\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\Big)^2-\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2\right].
 Hence a compact form is 
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta})=(\sigma^2-\tau)\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 + \tau\Big(\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\Big)^2.}

If the X_i were independent, then \tau=0 and we recover \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\theta})=\sigma^2\sum_i a_i^2.









Important 11: Proving Consistency I



Let X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathrm{Distribution}(\mu, \sigma^2) be a random sample such that each 
\operatorname{E}[X_i]=\mu,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X_i)=\sigma^2.
 Consider the following family of estimators of the mean \mu: 
\hat{\mu}_n = \frac{1}{n + c} \sum_{i=1}^n X_i,
 where c\in\mathbb{R} is a constant.
Show that \hat{\mu}_n is a consistent estimator of \mu for all c\in\mathbb{R}.


Solution

Bias: We have
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\mu}_n] &= \mathrm{E}\left[\frac{1}{n + c} \sum_{i=1}^n X_i\right] \\
&= \frac{1}{n+c} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[X_i] \\
&= \frac{1}{n+c} \sum_{i=1}^n \mu \\
&= \frac{n \mu }{n + c}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the bias is 
\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\mu}_n) = \frac{n \mu }{n + c} - \mu = \frac{n\mu}{n+c} - \frac{(n+c)\mu}{n+c} = -\frac{c \mu}{n + c}.

Variance: Due to independence of the X_i, we have
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Var}[\hat{\mu}_n] &= \mathrm{Var}\left[\frac{1}{n + c} \sum_{i=1}^n X_i\right] \\
&= \frac{1}{(n+c)^2} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{Var}[X_i] \\
&= \frac{1}{(n+c)^2} \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma^2 \\
&= \frac{n \sigma^2 }{(n + c)^2}.
\end{align*}
Consistency: To show consistency, we use Proposition 4.5. From the computed bias, we have (for any value of c\in\mathbb{R}): 
\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\mu}_n) = -\frac{c \mu}{n + c} \rightarrow 0, \quad \text{as } n\rightarrow \infty.
 Therefore, the estimator is asymptotically unbiased. Similarly, the variance converges to 0 for all c\in\mathbb{R}: 
\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\mu}_n) = \frac{n \sigma^2 }{(n + c)^2} \rightarrow 0, \quad \text{as } n\rightarrow \infty.
 Therefore, \hat{\mu}_n is a consistent estimator of \mu for all c\in\mathbb{R}.









Important 12: Computing Likelihoods I



Let X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathrm{Geometric}(p) be a random sample.
Derive the likelihood function.


Solution

For independent and identically distributed data, the likelihood function 5.1 takes the form 
L(p ; \underline{x}) = f(\underline{x} \mid p) = \prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i \mid p),
 where f(x_i\mid p) is PMF of the geometric distribution Geometric: 
f(x_i \mid p) = p(1-p)^{x_i - 1}.
 Therefore, the likelihood takes the form \begin{align*}
L(p; \underline{x}) &= \prod_{i=1}^n p(1-p)^{x_i - 1} \\
&= p(1-p)^{x_1-1}\times p(1-p)^{x_2-1}\times \ldots \times p(1-p)^{x_n-1} \\
&= p^n(1-p)^{\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - 1)} \\
&= p^n(1-p)^{n(\bar{x} - 1)},
\end{align*} where \bar{x} is the sample mean 4.1.









Important 13: Computing Likelihoods II



Let X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu, 1) be a random sample.
Derive the likelihood function.


Solution

For independent and identically distributed data, the likelihood function 5.1 takes the form 
L(\mu ; \underline{x}) = f(\underline{x} \mid \mu) = \prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i \mid \mu),
 where f(x_i\mid \mu) is PDF of the normal distribution Normal (with variance \sigma^2=1): 
f(x_i \mid \mu) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \exp\left\{ -\frac{(x_i - \mu)^2}{2} \right\}.
 Therefore, the likelihood takes the form \begin{align*}
L(\mu; \underline{x}) &= \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \exp\left\{ -\frac{(x_i - \mu)^2}{2} \right\} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \exp\left\{ -\frac{(x_1 - \mu)^2}{2} \right\} \times \ldots \times \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \exp\left\{ -\frac{(x_n - \mu)^2}{2} \right\} \\
&= (2\pi)^{-n/2} \exp\left\{ \sum_{i=1}^n -\frac{(x_i - \mu)^2}{2}  \right\} \\
&= (2\pi)^{-n/2} \exp\left\{ -\frac{1}{2 }\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2 \right\}.
\end{align*}









Important 14: Computing Likelihoods III



Suppose we have n i.i.d. log normal Lognormal observations 
X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathrm{LogNormal}(\mu, \gamma).

Derive the likelihood function.


Solution

For independent and identically distributed data, the likelihood function 5.1 takes the form 
L(\mu, \gamma ; \underline{x}) = f(\underline{x} \mid \mu, \gamma) = \prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i \mid \mu, \gamma),
 where f(x_i\mid \mu, \gamma) is PDF of the log normal distribution Lognormal (with \sigma^2 = \gamma): 
f(x_i \mid \mu, \gamma) = \frac{1}{x_i\sqrt{2\pi\gamma}} \exp\!\left(-\frac{(\log(x_i)-\mu)^2}{2\gamma}\right).
 Therefore, the likelihood takes the form 
\begin{align*}
L(\mu, \gamma; \underline{x}) &= \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{x_i\sqrt{2\pi\gamma}} \exp\!\left(-\frac{(\log(x_i)-\mu)^2}{2\gamma}\right) \\
&= (2\pi\gamma)^{-n/2} \exp\left\{-\frac{1}{2\gamma}\sum_{i=1}^n (\log(x_i) - \mu)^2\right\} \left(\prod_{i=1}^n x_i\right)^{-1}.
\end{align*}










Important 15: Fisher Information for a Binomial Observation



Suppose we have a single Binomial Binomial observation: 
X \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(n, \theta),
 Derive the expected Fisher information 5.7 \mathcal I(\theta) and the observed information 5.10 j(\theta;x). Evaluate both at the MLE when 0&lt;x&lt;n.


Solution

We compute \mathcal I(\theta)=-\mathrm E_\theta[\ell''(\theta;X)] and j(\theta;x)=-\ell''(\theta;x). The former averages over possible data; the latter is the curvature of the realised log-likelihood.
Step 1: Compute log-likelihood
Since we have a single observation, the likelihood is just the Binomial PMF: 
L(\theta ; x) = \binom{n}{x} \theta^x(1-\theta)^{n-x}.
 Therefore, the log-likelihood takes the form 
\ell(\theta; x) = \log L(\theta ; x) = \log\left(\binom{n}{x}\right) + x \log \theta + (n-x)\log(1-\theta).

Step 2: Compute Derivatives
We compute the first and second derivatives of the log-likelihood with respect to \theta: \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \ell(\theta; x) &= \frac{x}{\theta} - \frac{n-x}{1-\theta} \\
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2} \ell(\theta; x) &= -\frac{x}{\theta^2} - \frac{n-x}{(1-\theta)^2}.
\end{align*}
Step 3: Compute Fisher Information
We have 
\mathcal{I}(\theta) = -\mathrm{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2} \ell(\theta; X)\right] = -\mathrm{E}\left[-\frac{X}{\theta^2} - \frac{n-X}{(1-\theta)^2}\right],
 where the random component of the expectation is X\sim\mathrm{Binomial}(n, \theta). Therefore, manipulating the expectation: \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}(\theta) &= \mathrm{E}\left[\frac{X}{\theta^2} + \frac{n-X}{(1-\theta)^2}\right] \\
&= \frac{1}{\theta^2}\mathrm{E}[X] + \frac{1}{(1-\theta)^2} \mathrm{E}[n - X] \\
&= \frac{n\theta}{\theta^2} + \frac{n - n\theta}{(1-\theta)^2},
\end{align*} using the fact that \mathrm{E}[X] = n\theta. Continuing: \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}(\theta) &= \frac{n}{\theta} + \frac{n}{1-\theta} \\
&= \frac{n(1-\theta) + n\theta}{\theta(1-\theta)} \\
&= \frac{n}{\theta(1-\theta)}.
\end{align*}
The observed information is therefore 
j(\theta;x)=\frac{x}{\theta^2}+\frac{n-x}{(1-\theta)^2}.

Step 4: Compute MLE
For 0&lt;x&lt;n, the interior MLE solves: \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \ell(\theta; x) &= 0 \\
\frac{x}{\theta} - \frac{n-x}{1-\theta} &= 0 \\
x(1-\theta) &= \theta(n-x) \\
x &= n\theta \\
\Rightarrow \hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}} &= \frac{x}{n}.
\end{align*}
Step 5: Evaluate both information measures at the MLE
The plug-in expected information is \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}(\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}) &=  \frac{n}{\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}(1-\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}})} \\
&= \frac{n}{x/n(1-x/n)} \\
&= \frac{n^3}{x(n - x)}.
\end{align*} In this model the observed information happens to agree at the MLE: 
j(\hat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}};x)
=\frac{x}{(x/n)^2}+\frac{n-x}{(1-x/n)^2}
=\frac{n^3}{x(n-x)}.
 This equality is model-specific; the two definitions should not be conflated.









Important 16: Fisher Information for Geometric Data



Suppose we have a random Geometric Geometric sample: 
X_1,\ldots, X_n \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathrm{Geom}(\theta).
 Derive the expected Fisher information 5.7 for one observation and for the whole sample. Also derive the observed information 5.10 for the realised sample and evaluate it at the MLE.


Solution

Since the observations are i.i.d., expected information is additive: \mathcal I_n(\theta)=n\mathcal I_1(\theta). We first work with the whole sample, then retain the realised curvature for the observed information.
Step 1: Compute log-likelihood
For i.i.d. Geometric data, we have \begin{align*}
L(\theta; \underline{x}) &= \prod_{i=1}^n \theta(1-\theta)^{x_i - 1} \\
&= \theta^n(1-\theta)^{\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - 1)} \\
&= \theta^n(1-\theta)^{n(\bar{x} - 1)}.
\end{align*} Therefore, the log-likelihood takes the form 
\ell(\theta; \underline{x}) = \log L(\theta ; \underline{x}) = n\log(\theta) + n(\bar{x}-1)\log(1-\theta).

Step 2: Compute Derivatives
We compute the first and second derivatives of the log-likelihood with respect to \theta: \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \ell(\theta; \underline{x}) &= \frac{n}{\theta} - \frac{n(\bar{x} - 1)}{1-\theta} \\
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2} \ell(\theta; \underline{x}) &= -\frac{n}{\theta^2} - \frac{n(\bar{x} - 1)}{(1-\theta)^2}.
\end{align*}
Step 3: Compute Fisher Information
We have 
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) = -\mathrm{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2} \ell(\theta; \underline{X})\right] = -\mathrm{E}\left[-\frac{n}{\theta^2} - \frac{n(\bar{X} - 1)}{(1-\theta)^2}\right],
 where the random component of the expectation is X_i\sim\mathrm{Geom}(\theta). Therefore, manipulating the expectation: \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) &= \mathrm{E}\left[\frac{n}{\theta^2} + \frac{n(\bar{X} - 1)}{(1-\theta)^2}\right] \\
&= \frac{n}{\theta^2} + \frac{n}{(1-\theta)^2} \mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] - \frac{n}{(1-\theta)^2} \\
&= \frac{n}{\theta^2} + \frac{n}{(1-\theta)^2} \frac{1}{\theta} - \frac{n}{(1-\theta)^2},
\end{align*} where we used the fact that 
\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[X_i] = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\theta} = \frac{1}{\theta},
 and \mathrm{E}[X_i] = \tfrac{1}{\theta}. Continuing: \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) &= \frac{n (1-\theta)^2}{\theta^2(1-\theta)^2} + \frac{n\theta}{\theta^2(1-\theta)^2}  - \frac{n\theta^2}{\theta^2(1-\theta)^2}\\
&= \frac{n(1-\theta) +n\theta}{\theta^2(1-\theta)} \\
&= \frac{n}{\theta^2(1-\theta)}.
\end{align*} This immediately implies the Fisher information for a single observation is 
\mathcal{I}_1(\theta) = \frac{1}{\theta^2(1-\theta)}.

For the realised sample, the observed information is 
j_n(\theta;\underline x)
=-\ell''(\theta;\underline x)
=\frac{n}{\theta^2}
+\frac{n(\bar x-1)}{(1-\theta)^2}.
 For one realised observation x, this reduces to j_1(\theta;x)=\theta^{-2}+(x-1)(1-\theta)^{-2}.
Step 4: Compute MLE
The interior MLE solves: \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \ell(\theta; \underline{x}) &= 0 \\
\frac{n}{\theta} - \frac{n(\bar{x} - 1)}{1-\theta} &= 0 \\
n(1-\theta) &= n \theta (\bar{x} - 1) \\
1 &= \theta \bar{x} \\
\Rightarrow \hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}} &= \frac{1}{\bar{x}}.
\end{align*}
Step 5: Evaluate the information at the MLE
The plug-in expected information is \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_n(\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}) &=  \frac{n}{\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}^2(1-\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}})} \\
&= \frac{n}{\bar{x}^{-2}(1-\bar{x}^{-1})}. \\
&= \frac{n \bar{x}^3}{\bar{x} - 1}.
\end{align*} Substitution in the realised curvature gives the same value here: 
j_n(\hat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}};\underline x)
=\frac{n\bar x^3}{\bar x-1}.
 The corresponding average information per observation is this quantity divided by n; it is not the observed information from one separately observed Geometric variable.









Important 17: Fisher Information of the Variance of Normal Data



Suppose we have a random Normal Normal sample: 
X_1,\ldots, X_n \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu, \gamma),
 where \mu is a known value and \gamma is unknown.
Derive the expected Fisher information 5.7 and the observed information 5.10 for \gamma using the whole random sample. Evaluate both at the MLE.


Solution

We compute \mathcal I_n(\gamma)=-\mathrm E_\gamma[\ell''(\gamma;\underline X)] and j_n(\gamma;\underline x)=-\ell''(\gamma;\underline x) separately.
Step 1: Compute log-likelihood
Since we have n i.i.d. \mathcal{N}(\mu, \gamma) with \mu known, the likelihood function is 
\begin{align*}
L(\gamma;\underline{x}) &= \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\gamma}} \exp\left\{-\frac{(x_i - \mu)^2}{2\gamma}\right\} \\
&= (2 \pi)^{-n/2} \gamma^{-n/2} \exp\left\{-\frac{1}{2\gamma}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2\right\}.
\end{align*}
 Therefore, the log-likelihood takes the form 
\ell(\gamma; \underline{x}) = -\frac{n}{2}\log(2\pi) - \frac{n}{2}\log(\gamma) - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2.

Step 2: Compute Derivatives
We compute the first and second derivatives of the log-likelihood with respect to \gamma: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \gamma} \ell(\gamma; \underline{x}) &= -\frac{n}{2\gamma} + \frac{1}{2\gamma^2}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2,  \\
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \gamma^2} \ell(\gamma; \underline{x}) &= \frac{n}{2\gamma^2} - \frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2.
\end{align*}
 Step 3: Compute Fisher Information
We have 
\mathcal{I}(\gamma) = -\mathrm{E}\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\gamma^2} \ell(\gamma; \underline{X})\right] = -\mathrm{E}\left[\frac{n}{2\gamma^2} - \frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \mu)^2\right],
 where the random component of the expectation is the random data X_i\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu, \gamma). Therefore, manipulating the expectation: 
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}(\gamma) &=  -\frac{n}{2\gamma^2} + \frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[(X_i - \mu)^2] \\
&= -\frac{n}{2\gamma^2} + \frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n \gamma,
\end{align*}
 using the fact that each X_i - \mu \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \gamma) and so \mathrm{E}[(X_i - \mu)^2] = \mathrm{Var}(X_i) = \gamma. Continuing: 
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}(\gamma) &= -\frac{n}{2\gamma^2} + \frac{n\gamma}{\gamma^3} \\
&= \frac{n}{2\gamma^2}
\end{align*}

For the realised data, the observed information is 
j_n(\gamma;\underline x)
=-\frac{n}{2\gamma^2}
+\frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\mu)^2.

Step 4: Compute MLE
The interior MLE solves: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \gamma} \ell(\gamma; \underline{x}) &= 0 \\
-\frac{n}{2\gamma} + \frac{1}{2\gamma^2}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2 &= 0 \\
\Rightarrow \hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}} &= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2
\end{align*}

Step 5: Evaluate the information at the MLE
The plug-in expected information is 
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}(\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}}) &= \frac{n}{2\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}}^2} \\
&= \frac{n^3}{2\left(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2\right)^2}.
\end{align*}
 Because \sum_i(x_i-\mu)^2=n\hat\gamma_{\mathrm{MLE}}, evaluating the observed information also gives 
j_n(\hat\gamma_{\mathrm{MLE}};\underline x)
=-\frac{n}{2\hat\gamma_{\mathrm{MLE}}^2}
+\frac{n\hat\gamma_{\mathrm{MLE}}}{\hat\gamma_{\mathrm{MLE}}^3}
=\frac{n}{2\hat\gamma_{\mathrm{MLE}}^2}.










Important 18: Asymptotic Posterior for the Variance Parameter



Suppose we have an independent and identically distributed sample 
\underline{x} = (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)^\top
 from a distribution with PDF 
f(x \mid \gamma) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \gamma}}\exp\!\left[-\frac{x^2}{2\gamma}\right],
\qquad \gamma &gt; 0.

Determine the asymptotic posterior distribution for \gamma.


Solution

Step 1: Likelihood

f(\underline{x} \mid \gamma)
= \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \gamma}}
   \exp\!\left[-\frac{x_i^2}{2\gamma}\right]
= (2\pi \gamma)^{-n/2}
  \exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2\gamma}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2\right].

Step 2: Log-likelihood

\ell(\gamma; \underline{x})
= -\frac{n}{2}\log(2\pi)
  - \frac{n}{2}\log(\gamma)
  - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2.

Step 3: Derivatives

\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \gamma}
= -\frac{n}{2\gamma}
  + \frac{1}{2\gamma^2}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2,


\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \gamma^2}
= \frac{n}{2\gamma^2}
  - \frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2.

Let

a = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2.

Step 4: MLE
Solving \partial \ell / \partial \gamma = 0:

-\frac{n}{2\gamma} + \frac{a}{2\gamma^2} = 0
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\hat{\gamma} = \frac{a}{n}
= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2.

Step 5: Fisher Information
For one observation:

\ell(\gamma)
= -\tfrac12\log(2\pi\gamma)
  - \tfrac{x^2}{2\gamma},
 and so 
\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \gamma^2}
= \frac{1}{2\gamma^2} - \frac{x^2}{\gamma^3}.

Taking expectation under X \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\gamma):

\mathcal{I}(\gamma)
= -\mathrm{E}\!\left[\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \gamma^2}\right]
= -\left(\frac{1}{2\gamma^2} - \frac{\gamma}{\gamma^3}\right)
= \frac{1}{2\gamma^2}.

Thus for n observations:

\mathcal{I}_n(\gamma) = \frac{n}{2\gamma^2}.

Evaluated at the MLE:

\mathcal{I}_n(\hat{\gamma})
= \frac{n}{2\hat{\gamma}^2}.

Step 6: Asymptotic posterior
By Theorem 6.2

\gamma \mid \underline{x}
\approx
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
\hat{\gamma},\;
I_n(\hat{\gamma})^{-1}
\right)
=
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
\hat{\gamma},\;
\frac{2\hat{\gamma}^2}{n}
\right).

That is,

\boxed{
\gamma \mid \underline{x}
\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2,\;
\frac{2}{n}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2\right)^2
\right).
}










Important 19: Asymptotic Posterior for Poisson Data



Suppose we have independent Poisson Poisson observations 
X_1,\ldots, X_n \mid \lambda \sim \mathrm{Pois}(\lambda)


Determine the general asymptotic posterior distribution 6.2 for \lambda.
Suppose we observe n = 100 data points with sample mean 
\bar{x} = 3.21.
 Compute the asymptotic posterior distribution.



Solution


General Asymptotic Posterior.

Log-Likelihood: Since each observation is independent X_i \mid \lambda \sim \mathrm{Pois}(\lambda), we have 
\begin{align*}
f(\underline{x} \mid \lambda) &= \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{\lambda^{x_i} e^{-\lambda}}{x_i!} \\
&= \lambda^{n\bar{x}} e^{-n\lambda} \left(\prod_{i=1}^n x_i! \right)^{-1}.
\end{align*}
 Therefore, the log-likelihood is 
\log f(\underline{x} \mid \lambda) = n\bar{x} \log(\lambda) - n\lambda - \sum_{i=1}^n \log(x_i!).

Derivatives: The derivatives of the log-likelihood are 
   \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda} \log f(\underline{x} \mid \lambda) &= \frac{n\bar{x}}{\lambda} - n \\
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \lambda^2} \log f(\underline{x} \mid \lambda) &= -\frac{n\bar{x}}{\lambda^2}.
   \end{align*}

MLE: Solving 
   \frac{n\bar{x}}{\lambda} - n = 0
    yields the maximum likelihood estimator 
   \hat{\lambda} = \bar{X}.

Fisher Information: The Fisher information 5.7 for the whole sample is thus 
   \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_n(\lambda) &= -\mathrm{E}\left[-\frac{n\bar{X}}{\lambda^2}\right] \\
&= \frac{n}{n\lambda^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\mathrm{E}\left[X_i\right] \\
&= \frac{n}{\lambda},
   \end{align*}
    since each \mathrm{E}[X_i] = \lambda.
Plug in the MLE: The expected Fisher information evaluated at the MLE is 
   \mathcal{I}_n(\hat{\lambda}) = \frac{n}{\bar{x}}.
    (For the Poisson model, the observed information evaluated at the MLE happens to have the same value.)
Asymptotic Posterior Distribution: The asymptotic posterior distribution 6.2 is thus 
   \lambda \mid \underline{x} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\left(\bar{x}, \frac{\bar{x}}{n}\right)


Plug in data. Using the derived result from the first part and the provided summaries of the data n = 100 and \bar{x} = 3.21, we have 
\lambda \mid \underline{x} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\left(3.21, 0.0321\right)











Important 20: Asymptotic Posterior for Exponential Data



Suppose we have independent Exponential observations with rate parameter \lambda: 
X_1,\ldots,X_n \mid \lambda \sim \mathrm{Exp}(\lambda).


Determine the general asymptotic posterior distribution 6.2 for \lambda.
Suppose we observe n = 200 data points with sample mean 
\bar{x} = 7.14.
 Compute the asymptotic posterior distribution.



Solution


General Asymptotic Posterior

Likelihood and log-likelihood

The density of each X_i is 
f(x_i \mid \lambda) = \lambda e^{-\lambda x_i}.

Thus the likelihood is 
f(\underline{x} \mid \lambda)
= \prod_{i=1}^n \lambda e^{-\lambda x_i}
= \lambda^n \exp\!\left(-\lambda\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\right).

The log-likelihood is 
\ell(\lambda; \underline{x})
= n\log\lambda - \lambda\sum_{i=1}^n x_i
= n\log\lambda - n\lambda \bar{x}.


Derivatives


\frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda} \ell(\lambda; \underline{x})
= \frac{n}{\lambda} - n\bar{x},
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \lambda^2} \ell(\lambda; \underline{x})
= -\frac{n}{\lambda^2}.


MLE

Solve 
\frac{n}{\lambda} - n\bar{x} = 0,
 which gives the maximum likelihood estimator 
\hat{\lambda} = \frac{1}{\bar{X}}.


Fisher information

The Fisher information 5.7 for the whole sample is thus 
\mathcal{I}_n(\lambda) = -\mathrm{E}\left[-\frac{n}{\lambda^2}\right] = \frac{n}{\lambda^2}.

Evaluating the expected Fisher information at the MLE gives 
\mathcal{I}_n(\hat{\lambda})
= \frac{n}{\hat{\lambda}^2}
= n\bar{x}^2.
 Here the observed information is j_n(\lambda;\underline x)=n/\lambda^2, so it agrees with expected information for every \lambda.

Asymptotic posterior distribution

By Theorem 6.2 
\lambda \mid \underline{x}
\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\!\left(
  \hat{\lambda},\;
  \mathcal{I}_n(\hat{\lambda})^{-1}
\right)
= \mathcal{N}\!\left(
  \frac{1}{\bar{x}},\;
  \frac{1}{n\bar{x}^{2}}
\right).

Plugging in data
For n = 200 and \bar{x} =  7.14, 
\hat{\lambda} = \frac{1}{ 7.14} \approx 0.14,
 and the variance is 
\frac{1}{n\bar{x}^2}
= \frac{1}{200 \cdot 7.14^2}
\approx 9.807 \times 10^{-5}.

Thus the asymptotic posterior is 
\lambda \mid \underline{x}
\approx
\mathcal{N}\!\left(0.14,\; 9.807 \times 10^{-5}\right).











ImportantBayesian Inference for the Click-through Rate on Instagram



Software engineers at Meta are interested in the click-through rate \theta of a new method of displaying advertisements on Instagram. The click-through rate is the proportion of clicks out of the total number of impressions of an online advertisement.
They test the new method on users of their website, obtaining a total of 20 impressions. From prior experience, they specify \theta \sim \mathrm{Beta}(1,200).

Let X \mid \theta denote the number of clicks out of the total number of impressions. What is an appropriate model for X \mid \theta?

After running the test, the posterior obtained was 
\theta \mid x \sim \mathrm{Beta}(1,220).
 Determine the number of successful clicks x.

Construct a 90\% highest density interval (HDI) for \theta.

Hint: The following plot may be helpful.


Show code
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
from scipy.stats import beta

set_plot_theme("light")

theta_grid = np.linspace(0, 0.03, 100)
beta_pdf = beta.pdf(theta_grid, 1, 220)

plt.figure(figsize=(5,3))
plt.plot(theta_grid, beta_pdf)
plt.xlabel(r"$\theta$")
plt.ylabel("Density")
plt.title(r"PDF of $\theta \mid x \sim \mathrm{Beta}(1,220)$")
plt.xlim(0, 0.03)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()  

set_plot_theme("dark")

plt.figure(figsize=(5,3))
plt.plot(theta_grid, beta_pdf)
plt.xlabel(r"$\theta$")
plt.ylabel("Density")
plt.title(r"PDF of $\theta \mid x \sim \mathrm{Beta}(1,220)$")
plt.xlim(0, 0.03)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()  


















Solution

1. Likelihood model
An appropriate model is the Binomial: 
X \mid \theta \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(n=20,\ \theta).


2. Recovering x from the posterior
We have \begin{align*}
    \pi(\theta|x) &\propto \theta^x(1-\theta)^{20-x} \times \theta^0 (1-\theta)^{199} \\
    &\propto \theta^{x + 1 -1}(1-\theta)^{220-x - 1}.
\end{align*} The posterior being \theta \mid x \sim \textrm{Beta}(1, 220) implies x = 0.

3. A 90\% highest density interval for \theta
From the provided plot, a 90\% highest density interval would be of the form 0 &lt; \theta &lt; c, where c is the solution of the equation 
\int_0^c \pi(\theta|x)\,\mathrm{d}\theta = 0.9.
 Note that  \frac{\Gamma(220 + 1)}{\Gamma(1) \Gamma(220)} = \frac{220!}{219!} = 220.  Therefore, \begin{align*}
    \int_0^c \pi(\theta|x)\,\mathrm{d}\theta &= \int_0^c 220(1-\theta)^{219}\,\mathrm{d}\theta \\
    &= \left[-(1-\theta)^{220}\right]_{0}^c \\
    &= 1-(1-c)^{220}.
\end{align*} Therefore, we seek c such that \begin{align*}
    1-(1-c)^{220} &= 0.9 \\
    (1-c)^{220} &= 0.1 \\
    c  &= 1 - 0.1^{1/220} = 0.01041 \text{ (4 s.f.)}.
\end{align*} So, the 90\% highest density interval is \theta \in (0, 0.01041).









Important 21: Approximate MLE-based Confidence Interval for Geometric Data



Suppose we have n i.i.d. geometric Geometric observations 
X_1,\dots,X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathrm{Geometric}(p).


Using the asymptotic distribution of the MLE 5.9, construct a general 100(1-\alpha)\% approximate CI 7.5 for p.
Suppose we observe a random sample of size n = 100 from a geometric distribution, and the sample mean is \bar{x} = 9.84. Using the asymptotic normal approximation of the MLE, compute an approximate 95\% confidence interval for the parameter p.



Solution


Approximate CI. Note that, in order to compute the approximate CI we require both the MLE of p and the Fisher information.

Step 1: Compute Log Likelihood For i.i.d. geometric data, the likelihood is 
\begin{align*}
L(p; \underline{x}) &= \prod_{i=1}^n p(1-p)^{x_i - 1} \\
&= p^n(1-p)^{n(\bar{x} - 1)}.
\end{align*}
 The log-likelihood is thus 
\ell(p; \underline{x}) = n \log p + n(\bar{x} - 1) \log(1-p).

Step 2: Compute Derivates of Log-Likelihood The derivatives of the log-likelihood are 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial p} \ell(p; \underline{x})  &= \frac{n}{p} - \frac{n(\bar{x} - 1)}{1 - p}, \\
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial p^2} \ell(p; \underline{x})  &= -\frac{n}{p^2} - \frac{n(\bar{x} - 1)}{(1 - p)^2}.
\end{align*}

Step 3: Compute MLE We compute the MLE by solving 
\begin{align*}
\frac{n}{p} - \frac{n(\bar{x} - 1)}{1 - p} &= 0 \\
n(1-p) - n(\bar{x} - 1)p &= 0 \\
n\bar{x} p &= n \\
\Rightarrow \hat{p}_{\mathrm{MLE}} &= \frac{1}{\bar{x}}.
\end{align*}

Step 4: Compute Fisher Information The Fisher information for the whole sample is: 
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_n(p) &= -\mathrm{E}\left[-\frac{n}{p^2} - \frac{n(\bar{X} - 1)}{(1 - p)^2}\right] \\
&= \frac{n}{p^2} - \frac{n}{(1-p)^2} + \frac{n}{(1-p)^2}\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] \\
&= \frac{n}{p^2} - \frac{n}{(1-p)^2} + \frac{n}{p(1-p)^2},
\end{align*}
 since \mathrm{E}[X_i] = 1/p and so \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[X_i] = 1/p. Simplifying further, we obtain 
\mathcal{I}_n(p) = n\frac{(1-p)^2 - p^2 + p}{p^2(1-p)^2} = \frac{n}{p^2(1-p)}.
 Substituting p = \hat{p}_{\mathrm{MLE}} = 1/\bar{x} into the expected Fisher information gives 
\mathcal{I}_n(\hat{p}_{\mathrm{MLE}}) = \frac{n \bar{x}^2}{(1-1/\bar{x})} = \frac{n\bar{x}^3}{\bar{x} - 1}.
 For this model, the realised observed information evaluated at the MLE has the same value, but the two definitions are conceptually distinct.
Step 5: Compute Approximate CI The asymptotic distribution of the MLE 5.9 implies that, for large n, 
\hat{p}_{\mathrm{MLE}} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(p, \mathcal{I}_n(\hat{p}_{\mathrm{MLE}})^{-1}).
 Therefore, an approximate 100(1-\alpha)\% confidence interval takes the form: 
\frac{1}{\bar{x}} \pm z_{1-\alpha/2} \sqrt{\frac{\bar{x} - 1}{n \bar{x}^3}}.


Compute the Approximate CI using data. Using the derived formula from the first part 
  \frac{1}{\bar{x}} \pm z_{1-\alpha/2} \sqrt{\frac{\bar{x} - 1}{n \bar{x}^3}}.
   we plug in the given values \bar{x} = 9.84 and n=100. The relevant quantile is z_{0.975} = 1.96. Therefore, we obtain 
  0.1016 \pm 1.96×0.0096≈0.1016±0.0189.
   Therefore, an approximate 95\% confidence interval for p is 
\boxed{
   (0.083, 0.121), \quad \text{(to three decimal places)}.
}











Important 22: Asymptotic Distribution of the Exponential MLE



Suppose X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n is an i.i.d. random sample from an \mathrm{Exp}(\lambda) distribution, with density 
f(x \mid \lambda) = \lambda e^{-\lambda x}, \qquad x \ge 0.

The maximum likelihood estimator for \lambda is known to be 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}} = \frac{1}{\bar{X}}.
 Compute the approximate sampling distribution 5.9 of \hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}}.


Solution

Step 1: Recall the general asymptotic MLE result
From the asymptotic normality of the MLE, for regular models: 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
\;\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}\;
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
  \lambda,\;
  \mathcal{I}_n(\lambda)^{-1}
\right),
\quad \text{for large } n,
 where \mathcal{I}_n(\lambda) is the Fisher information for the whole sample.

Step 2: Compute the Fisher information
The log-likelihood is 
\ell(\lambda; \underline{x}) = n\log \lambda - n\lambda \bar{x}.

Differentiate: 
\frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda} \ell(\lambda; \underline{x})
= \frac{n}{\lambda} - n\bar{x},
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2 }{\partial \lambda^2} \ell(\lambda; \underline{x})
= -\frac{n}{\lambda^2}.

So the Fisher information for the whole sample is 
\mathcal{I}_n(\lambda) = -\mathrm{E}\left[-\frac{n}{\lambda^2}\right] = \frac{n}{\lambda^2}.

Thus 
\mathcal{I}_n(\lambda)^{-1}
= \frac{\lambda^2}{n}.


Step 3: Asymptotic distribution of the MLE
Plugging this into the general asymptotic MLE result: 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
\;\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}\;
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
  \lambda,\;
  \frac{\lambda^2}{n}
\right),
\quad \text{as } n \to \infty.

In practice, since \lambda is unknown, we use the plug-in \hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}} = 1/\bar{x} into the Fisher information, resulting in: 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
\;\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}\;
\mathcal{N} \left(
  \lambda,\;
  \frac{1}{n\bar{x}^2}
\right),










Important 23: Normal Data: Asymptotic Distribution of the MLE of the variance



Suppose 
X_1,\ldots, X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu, \gamma),
 with mean \mu known.
The maximum likelihood estimator for \gamma is known to be 
\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2.
 Compute the approximate sampling distribution 5.9 of \hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}}.


Solution

Step 1: Recall the general asymptotic MLE result
From the asymptotic normality of the MLE, for regular models: 
\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
\;\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}\;
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
  \gamma,\;
  \mathcal{I}_n(\gamma)^{-1}
\right),
\quad \text{for large } n,
 where \mathcal{I}_n(\gamma) is the Fisher information for the whole sample.

Step 2: Compute the Fisher information
The log-likelihood is 
\ell(\gamma; \underline{x}) = -\frac{n}{2} \log(2\pi\gamma) - \frac{1}{2\gamma}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2

Differentiate: 
\frac{\partial}{\partial \gamma} \ell(\gamma; \underline{x})
=  -\frac{n}{2\gamma} + \frac{1}{2\gamma^2} \sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2 }{\partial \gamma^2} \ell(\gamma; \underline{x})
= \frac{n}{2\gamma^2} - \frac{1}{\gamma^3} \sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2.

So the Fisher information for the whole sample is 
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_n(\gamma) &= -\mathrm{E}\left[\frac{n}{2\gamma^2} - \frac{1}{\gamma^3} \sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \mu)^2\right] \\
&= -\frac{n}{2\gamma^2} + \frac{1}{\gamma^3}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[(X_i - \mu)^2] \\
&= \frac{n}{2\gamma^2},
\end{align*}
 since \mathrm{E}[(X_i - \mu)^2] = \mathrm{Var}(X_i) = \gamma.
Thus 
\mathcal{I}_n(\lambda)^{-1} = \frac{2\gamma^2}{n}.


Step 3: Asymptotic distribution of the MLE
Plugging this into the general asymptotic MLE result: 
\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
\;\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}\;
\mathcal{N}\!\left(
  \gamma,\;
  \frac{2\gamma^2}{n}
\right).

In practice, since \gamma is unknown, we use the plug-in \hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}} =  \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2 into the Fisher information, resulting in: 
\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
\;\overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim}\;
\mathcal{N} \left(
  \gamma,\;
  \frac{2(\hat{\gamma}_{\mathrm{MLE}})^2}{n}
\right) = \mathcal{N} \left(
  \gamma,\;
  \frac{2\left(\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2\right)^2}{n^3}
\right),










Important 24: Large-Sample Approximate Confidence Interval for a Proportion



A university careers office surveys a random sample of n = 200 graduates to estimate the proportion who found full-time employment within 3 months of graduation. Out of the 200 respondents, 146 report being in full-time employment.
Assuming a Binomial model Binomial 
X \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(n = 200 , \,  p),
 where p is the true employment rate, use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to construct an approximate 95% confidence interval for p.


Solution

Step 1: Model and estimator
The sample proportion is 
\hat{p} = \frac{X}{n} = \frac{146}{200} = 0.73.

Step 2: Approximate distribution (CLT)
For large n, 
\hat{p} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\!\left(p,\, \frac{p(1-p)}{n}\right).
 Replacing p by \hat{p} gives the estimated standard error 
\mathrm{SE}(\hat{p}) = \sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})}{n}}
= \sqrt{\frac{0.73(0.27)}{200}} \approx 0.0317.

Step 3: Construct 95% confidence interval
Using the Normal quantile z_{0.975} = 1.96, 
\hat{p} \pm z_{0.975}\,\mathrm{SE}(\hat{p})
= 0.73 \pm 1.96(0.0317)
= (0.668,\, 0.792).

Step 4: Interpretation
We are approximately 95% confident that the true proportion of graduates in full-time employment within 3 months lies between 66.8% and 79.2%.









Important 25: Large-Sample Hypothesis Test for a Proportion



A public health agency claims that at least 70% of adults in a city receive an annual flu vaccination. To assess this claim, a random sample of n = 180 adults is surveyed. Of these, 117 report having received a flu vaccination in the past year.
Assuming a Binomial model Binomial 
X \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(n = 180,\, p),
 where p is the true proportion of adults who receive an annual flu vaccination, use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to perform a 5% significance level hypothesis test of the agency’s claim.


Solution

The sample proportion is 
\hat{p} = \frac{X}{n} = \frac{146}{200} = 0.73,

and, for large n, the CLT gives us 
\hat{p} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\!\left(p,\, \frac{p(1-p)}{n}\right).

Step 1: State the hypotheses
The claim “at least 70%” corresponds to 
H_0: p = 0.70, \qquad H_1: p &lt; 0.70.
 This is a one-sided (left-tailed) test.
Step 2: Compute the test statistic
The sample proportion is 
\hat{p} = \frac{117}{180} = 0.65.

Under the null hypothesis, the standard error is 
\mathrm{SE}_0 = \sqrt{\frac{p_0(1-p_0)}{n}}
= \sqrt{\frac{0.7(0.3)}{180}} \approx 0.0342.

The observed test statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}}= \frac{\hat{p} - p_0}{\mathrm{SE}_0}
= \frac{0.65 - 0.70}{0.0342}
\approx -1.46.

Step 3: Determine the rejection region / p-value
At the 5% significance level for a one-sided test, the critical value is 
z_{0.05} = -1.645.

Since 
-1.46 &gt; -1.645,
 the test statistic does not fall in the rejection region.
Equivalently, the p-value is 
\mathrm{Pr}(Z \le -1.46) \approx 0.072,
 which is greater than 0.05.
Step 4: Conclusion
There is insufficient evidence at the 5% significance level to reject the agency’s claim. The data do not provide strong enough evidence to conclude that fewer than 70% of adults receive an annual flu vaccination.









Important 26: Large-Sample Hypothesis Test for a Poisson Rate



A city council claims that the average number of pothole reports received per day is at least 12. To assess this claim, the number of pothole reports is recorded for a random sample of n = 50 days. Across these 50 days, a total of X = 540 pothole reports are logged.
Assuming a Poisson model Poisson 
X_i \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda), \qquad i=1,\dots,n,
 where \lambda is the true mean number of pothole reports per day, use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to perform a 5% significance level hypothesis test of the council’s claim.


Solution

Let X_1,\dots,X_n be the daily counts. The observed sample mean is 
\bar{x} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = \frac{540}{50} = 10.8.

For a Poisson random variable, \mathrm{E}[X_i]=\lambda and \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\lambda. For large n, the CLT gives 
\bar{X} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N} \left(\lambda, \frac{\lambda}{n}\right).

Step 1: State the hypotheses
The claim “at least 12 per day” corresponds to 
H_0: \lambda = 12, \qquad H_1: \lambda &lt; 12.
 This is a one-sided (left-tailed) test.
Step 2: Compute the test statistic
Under the null, the standard error is 
\mathrm{SE}_0 = \sqrt{\frac{\lambda_0}{n}}
= \sqrt{\frac{12}{50}} \approx 0.490.

The observed test statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \lambda_0}{\mathrm{SE}_0}
= \frac{10.8 - 12}{0.490}
\approx -2.45.

Step 3: Determine the rejection region / p-value
At the 5% significance level for a one-sided test, the critical value is 
z_{0.05} = -1.645.

Since 
-2.45 &lt; -1.645,
 the test statistic falls in the rejection region.
Equivalently, the p-value is 
\mathrm{Pr}(Z \le -2.45) \approx 0.007.

Step 4: Conclusion
There is sufficient evidence at the 5% significance level to reject the council’s claim. The data provide evidence that the true mean number of pothole reports per day is less than 12.









Important 27: Large-Sample Two-Sided Hypothesis Test for a Poisson Rate



A call centre manager claims that the average number of customer complaint calls received per hour is 5. To assess this claim, the number of complaint calls is recorded over a random sample of n = 60 one-hour periods. Across these 60 hours, a total of X = 360 complaint calls are received.
Assuming a Poisson model Poisson 
X_i \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda), \qquad i=1,\dots,n,
 where \lambda is the true mean number of complaint calls per hour, use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to perform a 1% significance level two-sided hypothesis test of the manager’s claim.


Solution

Let X_1,\dots,X_n be the hourly counts. The sample mean is 
\bar{X} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i = \frac{360}{60} = 6.

For a Poisson random variable, \mathrm{E}[X_i]=\lambda and \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\lambda. For large n, the CLT gives 
\bar{X} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\left(\lambda, \frac{\lambda}{n}\right).

Step 1: State the hypotheses
The claim “\lambda = 5 per hour” corresponds to 
H_0: \lambda = 5, \qquad H_1: \lambda \neq 5.
 This is a two-sided test.
Step 2: Compute the test statistic
Under the null, the standard error is 
\mathrm{SE}_0 = \sqrt{\frac{\lambda_0}{n}}
= \sqrt{\frac{5}{60}} \approx 0.289.

The observed test statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \lambda_0}{\mathrm{SE}_0}
= \frac{6 - 5}{0.289}
\approx 3.46.

Step 3: Determine the rejection region / p-value
At the 1% significance level for a two-sided test, the critical values are 
\pm z_{0.995} = \pm 2.576.

Since 
|3.46| &gt; 2.576,
 the test statistic falls in the rejection region.
Equivalently, the p-value is 
2\,\mathrm{Pr}(Z \ge 3.46) \approx 2(0.00027) \approx 0.00054.

Step 4: Conclusion
There is sufficient evidence at the 1% significance level to reject the manager’s claim. The data provide evidence that the true mean number of complaint calls per hour is different from 5.









Important 28: Large-Sample Two-Sided Hypothesis Test for an Exponential Rate



A hospital administrator claims that the average waiting time for a non-urgent blood test is 20 minutes. To assess this, a random sample of n = 64 waiting times is collected. The sample mean waiting time is \bar{X} = 24.0 minutes.
Assuming an Exponential model Exponential 
X_i \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathrm{Exponential}(\lambda), \qquad i=1,\dots,n,
 where \lambda is the rate parameter (so that \mathrm{E}[X_i]=1/\lambda), use the Central Limit Theorem 4.1 to perform a 5% significance level two-sided hypothesis test of the administrator’s claim.


Solution

For an exponential random variable with rate \lambda, 
\mathrm{E}[X_i] = \frac{1}{\lambda},
\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(X_i) = \frac{1}{\lambda^2}.

For large n, the CLT gives 
\bar{X} \overset{\text{approx.}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}, \frac{1}{n\lambda^2}\right).

Step 1: State the hypotheses
The claim “mean waiting time is 20 minutes” is 
H_0: \frac{1}{\lambda} = 20
\quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad
H_0: \lambda = \lambda_0 = \frac{1}{20} = 0.05,
 with the two-sided alternative 
H_1: \lambda \neq 0.05.

Step 2: Compute the test statistic
Under the null hypothesis, the standard error of \bar{X} is 
\mathrm{SE}_0 = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n\lambda_0^2}}
= \frac{1}{\lambda_0\sqrt{n}}
= \frac{1}{0.05\sqrt{64}}
= \frac{1}{0.05\cdot 8}
= 2.5.

The observed test statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - 1/\lambda_0}{\mathrm{SE}_0}
= \frac{24 - 20}{2.5}
= 1.60.

Step 3: Determine the rejection region / p-value
At the 5% significance level for a two-sided test, the critical values are 
\pm z_{0.975} = \pm 1.96.

Since 
|1.60| &lt; 1.96,
 the test statistic does not fall in the rejection region.
Equivalently, the p-value is approximately 
2\,\mathrm{Pr}(Z \ge 1.60) \approx 2(0.0548) \approx 0.110.

Step 4: Conclusion
There is insufficient evidence at the 5% significance level to reject the administrator’s claim. The data do not provide strong evidence that the mean waiting time differs from 20 minutes.









Important 29: One-Sample Z-test and t-test for a Normal Mean



A materials scientist is studying the tensile strength (in MPa) of a newly developed polymer. From a random sample of n = 25 specimens, the observed sample mean strength is 
\bar{X} = 52.4 \text{ MPa}.

It is reasonable to assume that tensile strength measurements are normally distributed and independent: 
X \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2),
 where \mu is the true mean tensile strength.
The manufacturer claims that the mean tensile strength of the polymer is 
\mu_0 = 50 \text{ MPa}.

You are asked to assess this claim at the 5% significance level under two different assumptions.

(a) Assume that the population variance is known to be \sigma^2 = 16 MPa^2.
(b) Assume instead that the population variance is unknown, and the sample standard deviation is 
s = 4.5 \text{ MPa}.

In each case, perform an appropriate two-sided hypothesis test of 
H_0 : \mu = 50,
\qquad
H_1 : \mu \neq 50.



Solution

(a) Z-test (known variance)
Since \sigma^2 is known and the data are normally distributed, a one-sample Z-test is appropriate.
The standard error of the sample mean is 
\mathrm{SE} = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{4}{\sqrt{25}} = 0.8.

The observed Z-statistic is 
Z_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu_0}{\mathrm{SE}}
= \frac{52.4 - 50}{0.8}
= 3.00.

At the 5% significance level for a two-sided test, the critical values are 
\pm z_{0.975} = \pm 1.96.

Since 
|3.00| &gt; 1.96,
 we reject H_0.
Equivalently, the p-value is 
2\,\mathrm{Pr}(Z \ge 3.00) \approx 0.0027,
 which is less than 0.05.
Conclusion (Z-test): There is strong evidence that the mean tensile strength differs from 50 MPa.

(b) t-test (unknown variance)
When \sigma^2 is unknown and estimated from the data, we use a one-sample t-test.
The estimated standard error is 
\widehat{\mathrm{SE}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{4.5}{\sqrt{25}} = 0.9.

The observed t-statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu_0}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}}
= \frac{52.4 - 50}{0.9}
\approx 2.67.

Under H_0, this statistic follows a t-distribution with 
n - 1 = 24
 degrees of freedom.
At the 5% significance level for a two-sided test, the critical values are 
\pm t_{0.975,,24} \approx \pm 2.064.

Since 
|2.67| &gt; 2.064,
 we reject H_0.
The p-value is approximately 
2\,\mathrm{Pr}(T_{24} \ge 2.67) \approx 0.013,
 which is less than 0.05.
Conclusion (t-test): There is sufficient evidence to conclude that the mean tensile strength differs from 50 MPa.

Final remark
Both tests lead to rejection of H_0, but the t-test is more conservative, reflecting the additional uncertainty introduced by estimating the variance from the data.









Important 30: VO_2 Max: One-Sided One-Sample Z-Test and t-Test for a Normal Mean



A sports physiologist is evaluating whether a new training programme increases athletes’ VO_2 max (in mL/kg/min). From a random sample of n = 41 athletes who completed the programme, the sample mean VO_2 max is 
\bar{X} = 47.2 \text{ mL/kg/min}.

It is reasonable to assume the VO_2 max measurements are normally distributed and independent across athletes: 
X \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2),
 where \mu is the true mean VO_2 max after completing the programme.
Previous seasons’ data suggest the historical mean is 
\mu_0 = 45 \text{ mL/kg/min}.

You are asked to test whether the programme increases mean VO_2 max, using a one-sided test at significance level \alpha = 0.01, under two different assumptions.

(a) Assume the population variance is known to be \sigma^2 = 36 (so \sigma = 6).
(b) Assume instead the population variance is unknown, and the sample standard deviation is 
s = 6.4.

In each case, perform the appropriate hypothesis test of 
H_0 : \mu = 45,
\qquad
H_1 : \mu &gt; 45.



Solution

(a) Z-test (known variance)
With known \sigma, use the one-sample Z-test.
The standard error is 
\mathrm{SE} = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{6}{\sqrt{41}} \approx 0.937.

The observed Z-statistic is 
Z_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu_0}{\mathrm{SE}}
= \frac{47.2 - 45}{0.937}
\approx 2.35.

At significance level \alpha = 0.01 for a right-tailed test, the critical value is 
z_{0.99} \approx 2.326.

Since 
2.35 &gt; 2.326,
 we reject H_0.
Equivalently, the p-value is 
\mathrm{Pr}(Z \ge 2.35) \approx 0.0094,
 which is less than 0.01.
Conclusion (Z-test): There is sufficient evidence at the 1% level to conclude that the programme increases mean VO_2 max.

(b) t-test (unknown variance)
With unknown \sigma, use the one-sample t-test.
The estimated standard error is 
\widehat{\mathrm{SE}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{6.4}{\sqrt{41}} \approx 1.00.

The observed t-statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu_0}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}}
= \frac{47.2 - 45}{1.00}
\approx 2.20.

Under H_0, this statistic follows a t-distribution with 
n-1 = 40
 degrees of freedom.
At \alpha = 0.01 (right-tailed), the critical value is 
t_{40,,0.99} \approx 2.423.

Since 
2.20 &lt; 2.423,
 we do not reject H_0.
The p-value is approximately 
\mathrm{Pr}(T_{40} \ge 2.20) \approx 0.017,
 which is greater than 0.01.
Conclusion (t-test): There is insufficient evidence at the 1% level to conclude that the programme increases mean VO_2 max.

Final remark
The Z-test rejects while the t-test does not at the 1% level. This illustrates how accounting for uncertainty in the variance (via the t-test use of the sample variance) leads to a more conservative inference.









Important 31: Sodium Content: One-Sided One-Sample Z-Test and t-Test for a Normal Mean



A food manufacturer monitors the average sodium content (in mg) of a packaged soup product. Regulations require that the mean sodium content should not exceed 820 mg per serving. To check compliance, a random sample of n = 40 servings is tested, yielding a sample mean of 
\bar{X} = 790 \text{ mg}.

It is reasonable to model sodium content as independent normal observations 
X \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2),
 where \mu is the true mean sodium content per serving.
You are asked to test whether the product’s mean sodium content is below the regulatory limit, using a one-sided test at significance level \alpha = 0.01, under two different assumptions.

(a) Assume the population variance is known to be \sigma^2 = 10{,}000 mg^2 (so \sigma = 100 mg).
(b) Assume instead that the population variance is unknown, and the sample standard deviation is 
s = 110 \text{ mg}.

In each case, perform the appropriate hypothesis test of 
H_0 : \mu = 820,
\qquad
H_1 : \mu &lt; 820.



Solution

(a) Z-test (known variance)
With known \sigma, use the one-sample Z-test.
The standard error is 
\mathrm{SE} = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{100}{\sqrt{40}} \approx 15.81.

The observed Z-statistic is 
Z_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu_0}{\mathrm{SE}}
= \frac{790 - 820}{15.81}
\approx -1.90.

At significance level \alpha = 0.01 for a left-tailed test, the critical value is 
z_{0.01} \approx -2.326.

Since 
-1.90 &gt; -2.326,
 we do not reject H_0.
Equivalently, the p-value is 
\mathrm{Pr}(Z \le -1.90) \approx 0.029,
 which is greater than 0.01.
Conclusion (Z-test): There is insufficient evidence at the 1% level to conclude that the mean sodium content is below 820 mg.

(b) t-test (unknown variance)
With unknown \sigma, use the one-sample t-test.
The estimated standard error is 
\widehat{\mathrm{SE}} = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{110}{\sqrt{40}} \approx 17.39.

The observed t-statistic is 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = \frac{\bar{X} - \mu_0}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}}
= \frac{790 - 820}{17.39}
\approx -1.73.

Under H_0, this statistic follows a t-distribution with 
n-1 = 39
 degrees of freedom.
At \alpha = 0.01 (left-tailed), the critical value is 
t_{39,\,0.01} \approx -2.426.

Since 
-1.73 &gt; -2.426,
 we do not reject H_0.
The p-value is approximately 
\mathrm{Pr}(T_{39} \le -1.73) \approx 0.046,
 which is greater than 0.01.
Conclusion (t-test): There is insufficient evidence at the 1% significance level to conclude that the product’s mean sodium content is below the regulatory limit.

Final remark
Both tests lead to the same conclusion. The t-test is slightly more conservative due to the additional uncertainty in estimating the variance.









Important 32: MAP Estimation in a Defect-Rate Problem



A factory produces electronic components, each of which may be defective with some unknown probability p.
To assess the defect rate, engineers inspect a random sample of n = 40 components and find that

x = 7

are defective.
To incorporate previous experience, the quality-control team places a prior distribution

p \sim \mathrm{Beta}(a,b), \qquad a = 2,\; b = 8.

You may assume the data follow the model 
X \mid p \sim \mathrm{Binomial}(n,p).


Derive the posterior distribution \pi(p \mid x).
Compute the Maximum a Posteriori (MAP) estimator \hat{p}_{\mathrm{MAP}}.
Interpret your MAP estimate in the context of the defect-rate problem.



Solution


Posterior Distribution
The likelihood is 
f(x \mid p) = \binom{n}{x} p^{\,x} (1-p)^{\,n-x}.

The prior is 
\pi(p) = \frac{\Gamma(a+b)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)} p^{\,a-1}(1-p)^{\,b-1}.

Therefore the posterior (dropping constants not depending on p) is 
\pi(p \mid x) \propto p^{\,x+a-1} (1-p)^{\,n-x+b-1}.

This is recognised as a Beta distribution: 
p \mid x \sim \mathrm{Beta}(a + x,\; b + n - x)
= \mathrm{Beta}(2 + 7,\; 8 + 40 - 7)
= \mathrm{Beta}(9,\; 41).

MAP Estimator
For a \mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta) distribution with \alpha&gt;1 and \beta&gt;1, the mode is provided in closed form, yielding the MAP: 
\hat{p}_{\mathrm{MAP}}
= \frac{\alpha - 1}{\alpha + \beta - 2}.

Here \alpha = 9 and \beta = 41, so 
\hat{p}_{\mathrm{MAP}}
= \frac{9 - 1}{9 + 41 - 2}
= \frac{8}{48}
= \frac{1}{6}
\approx 0.1667.

Alternatively, we can derive this using the approach outlined here 6.4:
We have 
\pi(p \mid x) \propto p^{\,x+a-1} (1-p)^{\,n-x+b-1}.
 Taking logs: 
\log \pi(p \mid x) = (x + a - 1)\log p + (n - x + b - 1)\log(1 - p) + C,
 for some p-independent constant C.
Differentiating with respect to p: 
\frac{\partial}{\partial p} \log \pi(p \mid x)
= \frac{x + a - 1}{p} - \frac{n - x + b - 1}{1 - p}.

Setting this equal to 0 and solving for p: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{x + a - 1}{p} - \frac{n - x + b - 1}{1 - p} &= 0 \\[4pt]
\frac{x + a - 1}{p} &= \frac{n - x + b - 1}{1 - p} \\[4pt]
(x + a - 1)(1 - p) &= (n - x + b - 1)p \\[4pt]
x + a - 1 &= (n - x + b - 1)p + (x + a - 1)p \\[4pt]
x + a - 1 &= (n + a + b - 2)p \\[4pt]
\Rightarrow\quad \hat{p}_{\text{MAP}} &= \frac{x + a - 1}{n + a + b - 2}.
\end{align*}

Plugging in n = 40,\; x = 7,\; a = 2,\; b = 8 gives 
\hat{p}_{\text{MAP}}
= \frac{7 + 2 - 1}{40 + 2 + 8 - 2}
= \frac{8}{48}
= \frac{1}{6}
\approx 0.1667.

Interpretation
Based on both prior information and the observed data, the MAP estimator suggests that the defect probability is approximately 
\hat{p}_{\mathrm{MAP}} \approx 0.17.

This is slightly lower than the raw sample proportion

\frac{x}{n} = \frac{7}{40} = 0.175,
 reflecting the influence of the prior belief that defects are relatively uncommon.










Important 33: MAP Estimation for a Call-Arrival Rate



A customer support centre receives phone calls according to a Poisson process with unknown rate \lambda (calls per hour).
To model the long-run arrival rate, analysts place a prior distribution 
\lambda \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(a,b),
\qquad a = 3,\; b = 1.5,
 where we use the shape–rate parameterisation: 
\lambda \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(a,b)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\pi(\lambda) \propto \lambda^{a-1} e^{-b\lambda}.

Over a monitoring period of T = 10 hours, they observe a total of y = 47 calls.
Assume that, conditional on \lambda, the total number of calls in T hours follows 
Y \mid \lambda \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(T\lambda).


Derive the posterior distribution \pi(\lambda \mid y).
Compute the Maximum a Posteriori (MAP) estimator \hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MAP}}.
Interpret your MAP estimate in the context of the call-arrival rate (give its units and practical meaning).



Solution


Posterior Distribution
The likelihood for the total number of calls Y in T hours, given \lambda, is 
f(y \mid \lambda)
= \Pr(Y = y \mid \lambda)
= \frac{(T\lambda)^y e^{-T\lambda}}{y!}.

The prior is 
\pi(\lambda)
= \frac{b^a}{\Gamma(a)} \lambda^{a-1} e^{-b\lambda},
\qquad a = 3,\; b = 1.5,
 using the shape–rate parameterisation.
Therefore, the posterior density (dropping constants not depending on \lambda) is 
\pi(\lambda \mid y)
\propto
\lambda^{\,y} e^{-T\lambda} \cdot \lambda^{\,a-1} e^{-b\lambda}
= \lambda^{\,y+a-1} e^{-(T+b)\lambda}.

This is recognised as a Gamma distribution with updated shape and rate: 
\lambda \mid y \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(a + y,\; b + T)
= \mathrm{Gamma}(3 + 47,\; 1.5 + 10)
= \mathrm{Gamma}(50,\; 11.5).

MAP Estimator
For a \mathrm{Gamma}(\alpha,\beta) distribution (shape–rate) with \alpha &gt; 1, the mode is 
\lambda_{\text{mode}}
= \frac{\alpha - 1}{\beta},
 which is also the MAP estimator in this conjugate setting: 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MAP}}
= \frac{\alpha - 1}{\beta}.

Here \alpha = 50 and \beta = 11.5, so 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MAP}}
= \frac{50 - 1}{11.5}
= \frac{49}{11.5}
\approx 4.26 \;\text{calls per hour}.

Alternatively, we can derive this by maximising the log-posterior directly (as in the general MAP procedure 6.4):
From above, 
\pi(\lambda \mid y)
\propto \lambda^{\,y+a-1} e^{-(T+b)\lambda}.
 Taking logs, 
\log \pi(\lambda \mid y)
= (y + a - 1)\log \lambda - (T + b)\lambda + C,
 for some \lambda-independent constant C.
Differentiating with respect to \lambda: 
\frac{\partial}{\partial \lambda} \log \pi(\lambda \mid y)
= \frac{y + a - 1}{\lambda} - (T + b).

Setting this equal to 0 and solving: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{y + a - 1}{\lambda} - (T + b) &= 0 \\[4pt]
\frac{y + a - 1}{\lambda} &= T + b \\[4pt]
\lambda &= \frac{y + a - 1}{T + b}.
\end{align*}

Substituting y = 47,\; a = 3,\; T = 10,\; b = 1.5 gives 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MAP}}
= \frac{47 + 3 - 1}{10 + 1.5}
= \frac{49}{11.5}
\approx 4.26,
 consistent with the mode formula above.
Interpretation
The MAP estimate suggests that the underlying call-arrival rate is approximately 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MAP}} \approx 4.26 \;\text{calls per hour}.

For comparison, the MLE based only on the data is 
\hat{\lambda}_{\mathrm{MLE}} = \frac{y}{T} = \frac{47}{10} = 4.7 \;\text{calls per hour}.

The MAP estimate is slightly lower than the MLE, reflecting the influence of the prior belief
(encoded by the \mathrm{Gamma}(3,1.5) prior) that the call rate is somewhat smaller than what the current data alone would suggest.










Important 34: Unbiased Estimation and Efficiency in the Normal Model



Suppose that X_1, \ldots, X_n are independent \mathcal{N}(\mu, \theta) random variables, where \mu is a known constant and \theta is the unknown parameter of interest. Let \bar{X} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.

Let 
\hat{\theta} = \frac{1}{c}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X})^2
 be an estimator of \theta.

Determine the distribution of \bar{X} and hence show that
\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] = \mu and \mathrm{E}[\bar{X}^2] = \theta/n + \mu^2.
Show that \mathrm{E}[X_i^2] = \theta + \mu^2 for any i = 1, \ldots, n.
Using the fact that \mathrm{E}[X_i\bar{X}] = \theta/n + \mu^2 for any i, or by another method,
derive the value of c that ensures \hat{\theta} is unbiased.

Show that for any unbiased estimator \hat{\theta} of \theta, it holds that 
\mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}] \geq \frac{2\theta^2}{n}.

Let 
\hat{\theta}' = \frac{1}{n+2}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \mu)^2
 be another estimator of \theta.
Determine whether \hat{\theta}' has a lower mean-squared error (MSE) than any unbiased estimator of \theta.
Hint: If X \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \theta), then \mathrm{Var}[(X-\mu)^2] = 2\theta^2.



Solution

(1a)
Since \bar{X} is the sample mean of i.i.d. normals, it follows that
\bar{X} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \theta/n).
Hence: 
\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}] = \mu,
\qquad
\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}^2] = \mathrm{Var}[\bar{X}] + \mathrm{E}[\bar{X}]^2
= \frac{\theta}{n} + \mu^2.


(1b)
For any i, 
\mathrm{E}[X_i^2] = \mathrm{Var}[X_i] + \mathrm{E}[X_i]^2 = \theta + \mu^2.


(1c)
We have: 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]
= \frac{1}{c}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[(X_i - \bar{X})^2]
= \frac{1}{c}\sum_{i=1}^n \Big(\mathrm{E}[X_i^2] - 2\mathrm{E}[X_i\bar{X}] + \mathrm{E}[\bar{X}^2]\Big).

Substituting known expectations: 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]
= \frac{1}{c}\sum_{i=1}^n \big(\theta+\mu^2 - 2(\theta/n+\mu^2) + \theta/n + \mu^2\big)
= \frac{n-1}{c}\theta.

Thus, \hat{\theta} is unbiased if and only if c = n-1.

(2)
The likelihood is: 
L(\theta) = (2\pi\theta)^{-n/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2\theta}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\mu)^2\right].

Hence the log-likelihood: 
\ell(\theta) = -\frac{n}{2}\log(2\pi) - \frac{n}{2}\log(\theta)
- \frac{1}{2\theta}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\mu)^2.

Differentiating twice: 
\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \theta^2}
= \frac{n}{2\theta^2} - \frac{1}{\theta^3}\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \mu)^2.

The Fisher information is: 
\mathcal{I}(\theta)
= -\mathrm{E}\!\left[\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \theta^2}\right]
= \frac{n}{2\theta^2}.

By the Cramér–Rao inequality: 
\mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}] \ge \frac{1}{\mathcal{I}(\theta)} = \frac{2\theta^2}{n}.


(3)
Compute the bias: 
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}']
= \frac{1}{n+2}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[(X_i - \mu)^2]
= \frac{n\theta}{n+2},
\qquad
\text{Bias}(\hat{\theta}') = -\frac{2\theta}{n+2}.

Variance: 
\mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}']
= \frac{1}{(n+2)^2}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{Var}[(X_i - \mu)^2]
= \frac{2n\theta^2}{(n+2)^2}.

Hence: 
\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}')
= \mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}'] + \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}')^2
= \frac{2n\theta^2}{(n+2)^2} + \frac{4\theta^2}{(n+2)^2}
= \frac{2\theta^2}{n+2}.

Since 
\frac{2\theta^2}{n+2} &lt; \frac{2\theta^2}{n}
 for all n &gt; 0,
\hat{\theta}' has a smaller mean-squared error than any unbiased estimator of \theta.









Important 35: Likelihood Ratio Test for Extreme Weather Events



The number of extreme weather events observed over a year is denoted by x,
modelled as a Poisson random variable X \sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\theta).
We wish to test the hypothesis H_0: \theta = 10,
where \theta = 10 represents “standard weather conditions.”

Show that the maximum likelihood estimate for \theta is \hat{\theta} = x.
(You may assume that any local optimum is a global maximum.)
Suppose x = 15 extreme weather events are observed. Show that 
\max_{\theta \in \Theta} L(\theta; x) = \frac{15^{15} e^{-15}}{15!},
\qquad
L(\theta = 10; x) = \frac{10^{15} e^{-10}}{15!},
 where L(\theta; x) is the likelihood for \theta given x = 15.
Show that the likelihood ratio test statistic is 
\lambda(x) = e^5 \left(\tfrac{2}{3}\right)^{15},
 and compute T_{\mathrm{obs}} = -2\log \lambda(x) to 3 significant figures.
Is there sufficient evidence to reject H_0 at the 10\% significance level?



Solution

1. Maximum likelihood estimate
Since we only have a single \mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda) observation, the likelihood is: 
L(\theta; x) = \frac{\theta^x e^{-\theta}}{x!}.
 The log-likelihood: 
\ell(\theta; x) = x \log \theta - \theta - \log(x!).
 Differentiating: 
\frac{\partial }{\partial \theta} \ell(\theta; x) = \frac{x}{\theta} - 1 = 0
\quad \Rightarrow \quad \hat{\theta} = x.
 Hence, the MLE is \hat{\theta} = x.

2. Evaluate the likelihoods
Given x = 15: 
\max_{\theta \in \Theta} L(\theta; x) = L(15; x) = \frac{15^{15} e^{-15}}{15!},
\qquad
L(\theta = 10) = \frac{10^{15} e^{-10}}{15!}.


3. Likelihood ratio statistic

\lambda(x) = \frac{L(10; x)}{L(15; x)}
= \frac{10^{15} e^{-10}}{15^{15} e^{-15}}
= e^5 \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{15}.
 Hence: 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = -2 \log \lambda
= -2[15 \log(2/3) + 5]
= 2.16 \quad (\text{3 s.f.}).


4. Hypothesis decision
By Wilk’s theorem, under H_0, the test statistic is approximately T \sim \chi^2_1.
The 10% upper-tail critical value is 2.71.
Since T_{\mathrm{obs}} = 2.16 &lt; 2.71, there is insufficient evidence to reject H_0 at the 10% level.
Therefore, we do not reject the hypothesis of standard weather conditions.









ImportantBias, Variance, and Mean Squared Error




Let \hat{\theta} be an estimator of a parameter \theta. Provide definitions for the following:

The bias of \hat{\theta}.
The variance of \hat{\theta}.
The mean squared error (MSE) of \hat{\theta}.

Is an unbiased estimator always preferred to a biased estimator? Justify your answer.
Prove that the mean squared error of \hat{\theta} is equal to its variance plus its squared bias: 
\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}) + \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})^2.




Solution

(1)

\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] - \theta

\mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}] = \mathrm{E}[(\hat{\theta} - \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}])^2]

\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}[(\hat{\theta} - \theta)^2]


(2)
An unbiased estimator is not always preferred.
If a biased estimator has a smaller MSE than an unbiased one, the biased estimator may be preferable because MSE accounts for both variance and bias.

(3)
Let \bar{\theta} = \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]. Then: \begin{align*}
\mathrm{E}[(\hat{\theta} - \theta)^2]
&= \mathrm{E}[(\hat{\theta} - \bar{\theta} + \bar{\theta} - \theta)^2] \\
&= \mathrm{E}[(\hat{\theta} - \bar{\theta})^2]
+ 2(\bar{\theta} - \theta)\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta} - \bar{\theta}]
+ (\bar{\theta} - \theta)^2.
\end{align*}
Since \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta} - \bar{\theta}] = 0, this simplifies to: 
\mathrm{E}[(\hat{\theta} - \theta)^2]
= \mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}] + (\bar{\theta} - \theta)^2
= \mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}] + \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})^2.

Thus, 
\boxed{\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}) + \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})^2.}










Important 36: Likelihood Ratio Test for the Lifespan of a Magical Artefact



The time (in years) until a magical artefact loses its power is modelled as an exponential random variable X \sim \mathrm{Exponential}(1/\theta), where \theta &gt; 0 is the mean lifespan.
According to archived lore, an authentic artifact should have mean lifespan \theta_0 = 50 years.
We wish to test the hypothesis

H_0 : \theta = 50

against the alternative that the true lifespan differs.

Show that the maximum likelihood estimate of \theta is \hat{\theta} = x, where x is the observed lifespan.
(You may assume that any critical point is a global maximum.)
Suppose a recovered artifact is observed to last x = 60 years. Show that 
\max_{\theta \in \Theta} L(\theta; x)
= \frac{1}{60} e^{-1},
\qquad
L(\theta = 50; x)
= \frac{1}{50} e^{-60/50},
 where L(\theta; x) denotes the likelihood under mean \theta.
Show that the likelihood ratio statistic is 
\lambda(x)
= \frac{5}{6} \, e^{-1/5},
 and compute the observed statistic T_{\mathrm{obs}} = -2 \log \lambda(x) to 3 significant figures.
Based on the asymptotic \chi^2_1 distribution of T_{\mathrm{obs}}, is there sufficient evidence to reject H_0 at the 10\% significance level?



Solution

1. Maximum likelihood estimate
Exponential density: 
f(x \mid \theta) = \frac{1}{\theta} e^{-x/\theta}, \qquad x \ge 0.
 Likelihood for a single observation: 
L(\theta; x) = \frac{1}{\theta} e^{-x/\theta}.
 Log-likelihood: 
\ell(\theta; x) = -\log \theta - \frac{x}{\theta}.
 Differentiate: 
\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \theta}
= -\frac{1}{\theta} + \frac{x}{\theta^2}
= 0
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
\hat{\theta} = x.


2. Evaluate the likelihoods
At the MLE \theta = 60: 
L(60; x)
= \frac{1}{60} e^{-60/60}
= \frac{1}{60} e^{-1}.
 At \theta = 50: 
L(50; x)
= \frac{1}{50} e^{-60/50}
= \frac{1}{50} e^{-6/5}.


3. Likelihood ratio statistic

\lambda(x)
= \frac{L(50; x)}{L(60; x)}
=
\frac{\tfrac{1}{50} e^{-6/5}}
     {\tfrac{1}{60} e^{-1}}
= \frac{60}{50} \, e^{-6/5 + 1}
= \frac{6}{5} e^{-1/5}.

Thus: 
\lambda(x) = \frac{5}{6} e^{-1/5}.

Then: 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = -2 \log \lambda(x)
= -2\left[\log(5/6) - \frac{1}{5}\right]
= 0.514 \quad (\text{3 s.f.}).


4. Hypothesis decision
Under H_0, T \sim \chi^2_1 approximately.
The 10\% upper-tail critical value is 2.71.
Since: 
T_{\mathrm{obs}} = 0.514 &lt; 2.71,
 we do not reject H_0 at the 10\% significance level.
There is insufficient evidence that the artifact’s lifespan differs from 50 years.









Important 37: Properties of the Maximum Likelihood Estimator



Let \underline{X} = (X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n) be a random sample from a continuous random variable X whose probability density function is given by 
f(x \mid \theta) =
\begin{cases}
\big(\theta(1 + x)^{\frac{1+\theta}{\theta}} \big)^{-1}, & x \ge 0, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
 where 0 &lt; \theta &lt; 1.
Answer the following:

Show that the log-likelihood can be written as

\ell(\theta \mid \underline{x}) = -n \log \theta - \frac{1+\theta}{\theta}\sum_{i=1}^n \log(1 + x_i).

Show that the maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) of \theta is

\hat{\theta} =
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \log(1 + x_i).

Using the fact that \mathrm{E}[\log(1 + X)] = \theta, show that \hat{\theta} is an unbiased estimator for \theta.
Is \hat{\theta} also an asymptotically unbiased estimator for \theta?



Solution

(a) Derivation of the log-likelihood
The likelihood function is

L(\theta; \underline{x}) = \prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i \mid \theta) = \theta^{-n} \prod_{i=1}^n (1 + x_i)^{-\frac{1+\theta}{\theta}}.
 Taking logs gives

\ell(\theta \mid \underline{x}) = -n \log \theta - \frac{1+\theta}{\theta}\sum_{i=1}^n \log(1 + x_i).


(b) Finding the MLE
Differentiate with respect to \theta:

\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \theta} = -\frac{n}{\theta} + \frac{1}{\theta^2}\sum_{i=1}^n \log(1 + x_i).
 Setting this equal to zero:

-\frac{n}{\hat{\theta}} + \frac{1}{\hat{\theta}^2}\sum_{i=1}^n \log(1 + x_i) = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \hat{\theta} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \log(1 + x_i).


(c) Unbiasedness
Given \mathrm{E}[\log(1 + X)] = \theta,

\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]
= \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[\log(1 + X_i)]
= \frac{1}{n} \cdot n\theta
= \theta.
 Hence, \hat{\theta} is unbiased.

(d) Asymptotic unbiasedness
Since \hat{\theta} is unbiased for every finite n,

\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] = \theta.
 Therefore, \hat{\theta} is also asymptotically unbiased.









Important 38: Estimation from a Uniform Distribution



Suppose that X_1, \ldots, X_n are independent random variables with X_i \sim U(\theta, 1), where \theta is an unknown parameter of interest.
Consider the estimator 
\hat{\theta} = \min(X_1, \ldots, X_n).


Suppose X \sim U(\theta, 1). Write down the CDF of X.
Show that the CDF of \hat{\theta} is given by 
F_{\hat{\theta}}(x) =
\begin{cases}
0, & x &lt; \theta, \\
1 - \left( \dfrac{1 - x}{1 - \theta} \right)^n, & \theta \le x \le 1, \\
1, & x &gt; 1.
\end{cases}

Derive the pdf f_{\hat{\theta}}(x) of \hat{\theta}.
Let Y \sim \mathrm{Beta}(1, n).
Show that for Z = (1 - \theta)Y + \theta, the pdf of Z is, for \theta &lt; z &lt; 1, 
f_Z(z) = n \frac{(1 - z)^{n - 1}}{(1 - \theta)^n},
 and hence conclude that f_{\hat{\theta}}(x) = f_Z(x) for all x \in \mathbb{R}.
Is \hat{\theta} a consistent estimator of \theta? Justify your answer.



Solution

(1)
The CDF of X \sim U(\theta, 1) is 
F_X(x) =
\begin{cases}
0, & x &lt; \theta, \\
\dfrac{x - \theta}{1 - \theta}, & \theta \le x \le 1, \\
1, & x &gt; 1.
\end{cases}


(2)
\begin{align*}
P(\hat{\theta} \le x)
&= 1 - P(\hat{\theta} &gt; x)
= 1 - P(X_1 &gt; x, \ldots, X_n &gt; x) \\
&= 1 - (1 - F_X(x))^n.
\end{align*}
For x \in (\theta, 1): 
P(\hat{\theta} \le x)
= 1 - \left( \frac{1 - x}{1 - \theta} \right)^n.

Hence, 
F_{\hat{\theta}}(x) =
\begin{cases}
0, & x &lt; \theta, \\
1 - \left( \dfrac{1 - x}{1 - \theta} \right)^n, & \theta \le x \le 1, \\
1, & x &gt; 1.
\end{cases}


(3)
Differentiating the CDF for x \in (\theta, 1): \begin{align*}
f_{\hat{\theta}}(x)
&= \frac{\partial}{\partial x} F_{\hat{\theta}}(x)
= n \frac{(1 - x)^{n - 1}}{(1 - \theta)^n}.
\end{align*}

(4)
Let Y \sim \mathrm{Beta}(1, n), and define Z = (1 - \theta)Y + \theta.
Then for z \in (\theta, 1): \begin{align*}
F_Z(z) &= P(Z \le z) = P(Y \le (z - \theta)/(1 - \theta)), \\
f_Z(z) &= \frac{1}{1 - \theta} f_Y\left( \frac{z - \theta}{1 - \theta} \right).
\end{align*}
Since f_Y(y) = n(1 - y)^{n - 1} for 0 &lt; y &lt; 1, \begin{align*}
f_Z(z)
&= \frac{1}{1 - \theta} n \left( 1 - \frac{z - \theta}{1 - \theta} \right)^{n - 1} \\
&= n \frac{(1 - z)^{n - 1}}{(1 - \theta)^n}.
\end{align*}
Thus, f_Z(z) = f_{\hat{\theta}}(z) for all z \in \mathbb{R}, so \hat{\theta} has the same distribution as Z.

(5)
Since Z \overset{d}{=} \hat{\theta}, we have \mathrm{E}[Z] = \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}] and \mathrm{Var}[Z] = \mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}].
Bias: \begin{align*}
\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]
&= \mathrm{E}[Z]
= \mathrm{E}[(1 - \theta)Y + \theta]
= (1 - \theta)\mathrm{E}[Y] + \theta \\
&= (1 - \theta)\frac{1}{n + 1} + \theta.
\end{align*}
Hence, 
\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = (1 - \theta)\frac{1}{n + 1}.

Variance: \begin{align*}
\mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}]
&= \mathrm{Var}[Z]
= (1 - \theta)^2 \mathrm{Var}[Y]
= (1 - \theta)^2 \frac{n}{(n + 1)^2 (n + 2)}.
\end{align*}
As n \to \infty, both the bias and variance tend to 0: 
\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}) = 0,
\qquad
\lim_{n \to \infty} \mathrm{Var}[\hat{\theta}] = 0.

Therefore, \hat{\theta} is a consistent estimator of \theta.









